\subsubsection{Aging Debt versus Technical Debt}
\label{sec:AD_vs_TD}


TD \citep{cunningham1992wycash} often arises from deliberate choices developers make in response to environmental pressures such as deadlines, limited resources, or other urgent necessities. For example, developers might implement quick fixes before a product launch, neglecting comprehensive documentation, or ignoring coding standards to meet pressing deadlines. Additionally, TD can stem from a lack of skill or understanding in implementing certain parts of software efficiently.  

AD identifies debt associated with maintaining, refactoring, and evolving software as it ages over time. This includes compatibility with legacy code, managing obsolete or deprecated code, keeping up with updates or upgrades, and managing non-maintained or unused code that may require future cleanup or adaptation. 
