 We now show that $\|L-f\|_{\infty}\leq \varepsilon$. Let $x\in[a,b]$. If $x=x_i$ for some $i$ then $L(x)=f(x)$. If $x\in (x_{i-1},x_i)$ for some $i\in\{1,\dots,m\}$, we distinguish two cases. If $g(x_i)-g(x_{i-1}^+)=0$, then $f$ is constant and equal to $f(x_{i-1}^+)$ on $(x_{i-1},x_i)$ and hence $|L(x)-f(x)|=0$. If $(x_{i-1},x_i)$ does not have zero $g$--measure, denote $\lambda = (g(x)-g(x_{i-1}^+))(g(x_i)-g(x_{i-1}^+))^{-1}\in[0,1]$, then,
