\subsection{Our results}

In this paper, we provide a negative answer to the above question by showing a tight query lower bound $\Omega((1-\epsilon)d^2)$. 
Therefore, our lower bound, combined with the upper bound $O(d^2)$ by the exact unitary time-reversal protocol given in \cite{chen2024quantum}, fully settles the query complexity of the unitary time-reversal task.
Formally, our main result is as follows:
\begin{theorem}[\cref{thm-640254} and \cref{coro-6290408} restated]
\label{thm-6291832}
 Any protocol that solves the unitary time-reversal task to within diamond norm or average-case distance\footnote{The average-case distance is given in \cref{def-6290002}.} error $\epsilon$, must use at least $\Omega((1-\epsilon)d^2)$ queries to the unknown $d$-dimensional unitary.
\end{theorem}


One interesting aspect of our result is the error scaling $(1-\epsilon)$, which fundamentally differs from the typical scaling $\poly(1/\epsilon)$ of learning or metrology tasks.
This error scaling is natural as the unitary time-reversal can be done exactly (i.e., $\epsilon=0$).
Thus, \Cref{thm-6291832} provides a \textit{robust and optimal} hardness guarantee: allowing (even to constant-level) error cannot lead to better efficiency.
In comparison, most prior works focus on the lower bounds only for the \textit{exact} case (i.e., $\epsilon=0$),
which has recently been settled by Odake, Yoshida, and Murao~\cite{odake2024analytical} through a differentiation-based SDP framework (see further comparison in \cref{sec-710433,tab:main}).
We note that their methods cannot provide hardness guarantee in the non-exact regime: it cannot rule out the possibility that a non-exact protocol may achieve better efficiency.
It is also worth mentioning that our lower bound holds not only for diamond norm error $\epsilon$ (i.e., worst-case distinguishability), but also for an average-case distance error $\epsilon$ (i.e., average-case distinguishability over Haar random input states), where the latter generally represents a much stronger form of hardness.

\paragraph{Overview of techniques.}
Here, we briefly outline the main idea for the proof of \cref{thm-6291832}.
Suppose $R$ is a protocol for the unitary time-reversal task that uses $n$ queries to the unknown unitary $U$. Then, $R$ can be described by an $(n+1)$-comb~\cite{chiribella2008quantum,chiribella2009theoretical} on $(\mathcal{H}_0,\mathcal{H}_1,\ldots,\mathcal{H}_{2n+1})$ where each $\mathcal{H}_i$ is a copy of $\mathbb{C}^d$. 
We make an additional query to $U$ at the end of $R$, so that the overall channel approximates the identity channel
$\mathcal{I}_{\mathcal{H}_0\rightarrow \mathcal{H}_{2n+2}}$ from $\mathcal{H}_0$ to $\mathcal{H}_{2n+2}$ (see \cref{fig-6210108}).
Here, the $n+1$ queries to $U$ in total can be also described by an $(n+1)$-comb: 
\[C_U\coloneqq \kettbbra{U}{U}^{\otimes n+1}.\] 
Consequently, the overall channel is 
\(
R \star C_U\approx \kettbbra{I_{\mathcal{H}_0\rightarrow\mathcal{H}_{2n+2}}}{I_{\mathcal{H}_0\rightarrow\mathcal{H}_{2n+2}}},
\)
where $\star$ is the link product~\cite{chiribella2009theoretical} for quantum combs and $\kettbbra{I_{\mathcal{H}_0\rightarrow\mathcal{H}_{2n+2}}}{I_{\mathcal{H}_0\rightarrow\mathcal{H}_{2n+2}}}$ is the Choi representation of the identity channel $\mathcal{I}_{\mathcal{H}_0\rightarrow\mathcal{H}_{2n+2}}$. 
Taking Haar expectation over $U\in\mathbb{U}_d$ and using the bilinearity of $\star$, we obtain:
\begin{equation}\label{eq-721708}
R \star \E_U[C_U] \approx \kettbbra{I_{\mathcal{H}_0\rightarrow\mathcal{H}_{2n+2}}}{I_{\mathcal{H}_0\rightarrow\mathcal{H}_{2n+2}}},
\end{equation}
where $\E_U[C_U]$ is the $(n+1)$-th moment of the Haar random unitary under Choi representation (also called performance operator~\cite{quintino2022deterministic} for the time-reversal task).
Then, we define the \textit{stair operators} $\{A_k\}_{k=1}^n$ (see \cref{def-6100203}) which are used to analyze the Haar moment $\E_U[C_U]$.
The stair operators are defined in the Young-Yamanouchi basis~\cite{ceccherini2010representation,harrow2005applications} under the Schur-Weyl duality of an asymmetric bipartite quantum system.
We prove two lemmas (see \cref{lemma-641548} and \cref{lemma-641551}) for the stair operators. 
The first lemma shows that they provide a good upper bound (w.r.t. the L\"owner order) for the Haar moment:
\begin{equation}\label{eq-721713}
\E_U[C_U]\sqsubseteq I_{\mathcal{H}_{2n+1}}\otimes A_n,
\end{equation}
where $I_{\mathcal{H}_{2n+1}}$ is the identity operator on $\mathcal{H}_{2n+1}$.
The second lemma indicates that the stair operators are compatible with quantum combs through the link product $\star$, i.e., they can be sequentially contracted to identity up to certain coefficient when linked with an arbitrary $(n+1)$-comb $R$:
\begin{equation}\label{eq-721714}
R\star (I_{\mathcal{H}_{2n+1}}\otimes A_n)=\frac{n+1}{d} I_{\mathcal{H}_{2n+2}}\otimes I_{\mathcal{H}_0}.
\end{equation}
Using these two lemmas, we can show
\begin{equation}\label{eq-750225}
R\star \E_U[C_U]\sqsubseteq R\star(I_{\mathcal{H}_{2n+1}}\otimes A_n)=\frac{n+1}{d}I_{\mathcal{H}_{2n+2}}\otimes I_{\mathcal{H}_0},
\end{equation}
where we use the property that $\star$ preserves the L\"owner order.
Intuitively, this means if $n$ is small and $U$ is Haar random, the overall channel $R\star \E_U[C_U]$ is highly depolarizing, for any protocol $R$.
Therefore, combining \cref{eq-750225} with \cref{eq-721708}, we can see that $n$ must be $\Omega(d^2)$ since the maximum eigenvalue of $\kettbbra{I_{\mathcal{H}_0\rightarrow\mathcal{H}_{2n+2}}}{I_{\mathcal{H}_0\rightarrow\mathcal{H}_{2n+2}}}$ is $d$.
As our proof is based on the Choi representation, it is natural to expect that our lower bound applies even for the average-case distance error, according to \cref{eq-6282338}.

To prove these two lemmas, we perform a representation-theoretic analysis of the stair operators.
Specifically, we exploit the raising and lowering techniques of Young diagrams based on the branching rule of symmetric group representations to show \cref{eq-721713} and \cref{eq-721714}, respectively. 
Since the subsystem to be traced out is not the ``last'' subsystem (w.r.t. the ordering fixed by the action of symmetric groups) in the stair operators, the lowering techniques are not directly applicable here.
To address this, we use the Young's orthogonal form~\cite{ceccherini2010representation} for adjacent transpositions to swap the last two subsystems and then analyze the resulting expressions.
By this, we can reduce the original problems to pure combinatorics on Young diagrams (see \cref{sec-722129}), which is then solved with the assistance of Kerov's interlacing sequences~\cite{kerov1993transition,kerov2000anisotropic}.

\paragraph{Lower bound for generalized time-reversal.}
Moreover, we can provide a lower bound for the \textit{generalized unitary time-reversal} task: approximately implement the time-reverse $U^{-t}=e^{-iHt}$ for a given reversing time $t>0$.
Here, $H$ is allowed to be any Hamiltonian satisfying $e^{iH}=U$ and $\norm*{H}\leq \pi$.
Using \cref{thm-6291832}, we can provide a tight query lower bound $\Omega(d^2)$ for this task (matching the upper bound $O(d^2)$ by unitary tomography~\cite{haah2023query}). Specifically, we have:

\begin{corollary}[\cref{cor:generalized-time-reversal} restated]
Any protocol that that solves the generalized time-reversal task, even for a constant reversing time $t>0$ and to within constant diamond norm error, must use at least $\Omega(d^2)$ queries to the unknown $d$-dimensional unitary.
\end{corollary}

\paragraph{Lower bound for unitary controlization.}
Our main result can be further used to derive a tight query lower bound for quantum unitary controlization.
Specifically, we consider the task called \textit{phase-oblivious unitary controlization}, i.e., adding quantum control to an unknown unitary $U$ with an unspecified relative phase (see \cref{def:quantum_control_implementation} for formal definition and discussion there about related work~\cite{AFCB14,gavorova2024topological,TMM+25} on unitary controlization). 
Since the global phase of $U$ has no physical effect, the task only requires to implement control-$U$ up to an arbitrary relative phase.
More specifically, it aims to implement $m$ unitaries:
\[V_1,\ldots,V_m \approx \ketbra{0}{0}\otimes I+e^{i\theta}\ketbra{1}{1}\otimes U,\]
for some $\theta$ not specified.
Note that $V_1,\ldots,V_m$ approximate the control-$U$ up to the same relative phase $\theta$.
From an operational point of view, this is the minimum requirement for the implemented unitaries $\{V_i\}_{i=1}^m$ to be then used in quantum algorithms like phase estimation~\cite{CEMM98}, quantum signal processing~\cite{LC17}, or quantum singular value transformation~\cite{gilyen2019quantum}. 
For this task, we prove a tight query lower bound (matching the upper bound $O(d^2)$ by unitary tomography~\cite{haah2023query}) as follows:

\begin{theorem}[\cref{thm:control-is-hard} restated]
\label{thm:main-2}
Any protocol that solves the phase-oblivious unitary controlization task, even for constant $m$ and to within constant diamond norm error, must use at least $\Omega(d^2)$ queries to the unknown $d$-dimensional unitary.
\end{theorem}

The hardness of unitary controlization in \Cref{thm:main-2} is essentially based on the hardness of unitary time-reversal in \Cref{thm-6291832}.
In particular, by a folklore use of the coherent phase estimation algorithm~\cite{CEMM98,NC10} together with a constant number of queries to control-$U$ (with unspecified relative phase), one can implement $U^{-1}$ to within constant error (see \Cref{lmm:control-to-inverse} for formal statement).
This observation, combined with the lower bound for unitary time-reversal, naturally yields a lower bound for unitary controlization.


