\subsubsection{Lower bounds from semidefinite programming}\label{sec-710433}
Quintino, Dong, Shimbo, Soeda, and Murao~\cite{quintino2019probabilistic,quintino2019reversing} developed a systematic semidefinite programming (SDP) approach to find probabilistic exact protocols (i.e., exact conditioned on a flag output bit being ``success'') for unitary transformation tasks such as transposition, complex conjugation and time-reversal.
Query lower bounds are established for such protocols;
in particular, $\Omega(d)$ queries are required for probabilistic exact unitary time-reversal. 
This approach is extended by Yoshida, Koizumi, Studzi{\'n}ski, Quintino, and Murao~\cite{YKS+24} to derive the same $\Omega(d)$ query lower bound but applicable to approximate unitary time-reversal.
Odake, Yoshida, and Murao~\cite{odake2024analytical} further developed a general framework for deriving lower bounds on deterministic exact unitary transformation tasks. Specifically, they consider transformations described by differentiable functions $f:\mathbb{U}_d\rightarrow \mathbb{U}_d$ and analyze the induced Lie algebra mappings obtained by differentiation at various points.
Within the Lie algebra framework, they formulate an SDP such that the number of queries required to implement $f(\cdot)$ is lower bounded by the SDP solution. Consequently, they show an $\Omega(d^2)$ query lower bound for deterministic exact unitary time-reversal, matching the upper bound $O(d^2)$ by the protocol in~\cite{chen2024quantum}.

\paragraph{Comparison to \cite{odake2024analytical}.}
The differentiation-based method in \cite{odake2024analytical} appears challenging to extend directly to the analysis of non-exact protocols, so that their lower bound $\Omega(d^2)$ only applies to exact unitary time-reversal protocols. 
This leaves open the possibility that non-exact (approximate) protocols may achieve better efficiency. 
Indeed, this possibility comes true when access to control-$U$ is available: a non-exact protocol based on phase estimation can approximately implement $U^{-1}$ to within error $\epsilon$ using only $O(\poly(1/\epsilon))$ queries (see \Cref{lmm:control-to-inverse}). 
One might wonder if this better efficiency is also achievable with access only to $U$. 
Our lower bound provides a negative answer to this question.
Furthermore, as our lower bound applies to non-exact protocols, it can go through the reduction by phase estimation (inherently non-exact) to yield a tight lower bound for unitary contorlization (see \cref{thm:control-is-hard}).

\subsubsection{Lower bounds from topological obstructions}
Gavorov{\'a}, Seidel, and Touati~\cite{gavorova2024topological} established lower bounds for unitary transformation tasks from a topological perspective. 
By proving any $m$-homogeneous function from $\mathbb{U}_d$ to the $1$-sphere $\mathcal{S}^1$ must satisfy $d\mid m$ (i.e., $d$ divides $m$), they show that any \textit{clean} protocol (i.e., the ancilla output must be returned in $\ket{0}$ assuming the protocol is only unitary with postselection) for unitary time-reversal task requires $\Omega(d)$ queries. Note that the cleanness requirement is a strong constraint, since uncomputation of ancillas is generally hard when the protocol has access only to the time-forward $U$. Moreover, they also show that the implementation of $\ketbra{0}{0}\otimes I+e^{i\theta(U)}\ketbra{1}{1}\otimes U$ for some pre-fixed phase function $\theta:\mathbb{U}_d\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$ cannot be done using finite number of queries to $U$. Note that this task has stronger requirement than our phase-oblivious unitary controlization task (see \cref{def:quantum_control_implementation}) as we do not specify a pre-fixed phase function.

\subsubsection{Lower bounds from quantum learning}
Cotler, Schuster, and Mohseni~\cite{cotler2023information} designed a quantum learning task such that when ancillas are not allowed: $\Omega(d^{1/4})$ queries to $U$ are required to solve it; and only $O(1)$ queries are sufficient given additional access to the time-reverse $U^{-1}$. This separation implies an $\Omega(d^{1/4})$ query lower bound for the unitary time-reversal task, but it applies only to protocols without ancillas~\cite{schuster2024random}.
Schuster, Haferkamp, and Huang~\cite{schuster2024random} later established a stronger separation without this limitation. Their new learning task leverages the low depth construction of pseudorandom unitaries~\cite{schuster2024random,ma2025construct} and the connectivity features of quantum dynamics~\cite{schuster2023learning}. Specifically, this task cannot be solved within any polynomial number of queries to $U$; but it can be solved with $O(1)$ queries given additional access to the time-reverse $U^{-1}$. This implies a lower bound of $\omega(\poly\log d)$ for unitary time-reversal.

%We note that both lower bounds are not tight for the time-reversal task. This is intuitive, as they are designed to establish separations in specific learning tasks rather than addressing the lower bound of the time-reversal task directly.

\subsection{Discussion}
In this paper, we settle the complexity of the unitary time-reversal, a fundamental task in quantum information processing.
We prove a tight query lower bound $\Omega((1-\epsilon)d^2)$ for this task.
Moreover, our result implies a lower bound $\Omega(d^2)$ for the unitary contorlization task, which is also tight with respect to the dimension. 
Here, we further discuss our results from three different aspects.

\paragraph{Hardness for low-depth unitaries.}
We note that the lower bound provided by \cite{schuster2024random} applies even to $O(\poly\log\log d)$-depth unitaries\footnote{A $t$-depth unitary is a unitary that can be implemented by a $t$-depth circuit using single- and two-qubit gates}. 
That is, any protocol, even if it only solves the time-reversal task for $O(\poly\log\log d)$-depth unitaries, must use at least $\omega(\poly\log d)$ queries. 
We remark that our result, when combined with the low-depth construction of pseudorandom unitaries~\cite{ma2025construct,schuster2024random}, can also give the same lower bound for $O(\poly\log\log d)$-depth unitaries. 
This is done by replacing the Haar expectation in \cref{thm-691837} with the expectation over the low-depth pseudorandom unitaries and then proving that this change will cause only negligible difference.
Moreover, when combined with the low-depth construction of unitary $t$-designs~\cite{schuster2024random}, our result implies a lower bound $\widetilde{\Omega}(t)$\footnote{$\widetilde{\Omega}(\cdot)$ hides polylogarithmic factors.} for the time-revesal of $t$-depth unitaries for any $t< d^2$. This can be viewed as a graded version of our lower bound. It is worth mentioning that these graded lower bounds are purely information-theoretic, thus does not rely on the cryptographic assumption required by pseudorandom unitaries~\cite{ma2025construct}.

\paragraph{General framework for unitary transformations.}
An interesting question is how to develop a general framework for the hardness of non-exact unitary transformations, like the SDP-based framework established by \cite{odake2024analytical} for the exact cases. 
In particular, what are the lower bounds for non-exact unitary transposition and complex conjugation? We conjecture that they both are $\Omega((1-\epsilon)d)$, given the existing lower bound $\Omega(d)$ for exact unitary transposition and complex conjugation by \cite{odake2024analytical,quintino2019probabilistic} and the upper bound $O(d)$ for unitary complex conjugation by \cite{miyazaki2019complex}.

\paragraph{Bound for high-precision unitary controlization}
In this paper, we provide a query lower bound $\Omega(d^2)$ for the unitary controlization which is tight with respect to the dimension. 
However, when considering non-constant error, the current best upper bound is $O(d^2/\epsilon)$ (for diamond norm error $\epsilon$), which is obtained from the optimal unitary tomography~\cite{haah2023query}.
Therefore, a question remains open: Is tomography optimal for this task when concerning non-constant error? Furthermore, can we establish a tight lower bound even for an average-case distance error $\epsilon$?


