\subsection{Usage}\label{sec:usage}
In general, there are flags\footnote{The flags differ by compiler/linker and will be explained in the paper's artifacts.} which must be added to load our plugin into the compiler and adjust its optimization pipeline.
%
The simplest way to use \tool{} is at the file or program scope.
This only requires an additional flag for the compilation command, e.g.: \verb|--raptor-truncate-all=64_to_5_14;32_to_3_8|.
%\begin{verbatim}
%  --raptor-truncate-all=64_to_5_14;32_to_3_8
%\end{verbatim}
The flag instructs \tool{} to perform a truncation on the currently compiled files for the instructed truncations (in this case \unit[64]{bit} width \fp{} operations to a type with \unit[5]{bit} exponent and \unit[14]{bit} mantissa, and analogously for \unit[32]{bit} width). If specified for all files, we achieve full-program truncation.
%
To use function scope truncation, the code needs to be instrumented as shown in \cref{lst:raptor_pass_op_mode_before}.
%\cref{lst:raptor_pass_op_mode_before} shows an example usage of the function scope truncation.
The user can request \tool{} for a truncated version of a function by using the \verb|_raptor_trunc_func_[op,mem]| functions, depending on the desired mode. The parameters specify the function and \fp{} width to be truncated alongside the target exponent/mantissa lengths\footnote{Currently, we require the exponent/mantissa to be compile-time constants.}. The returned value is a function with the same type as the input function.
%goes in limitations: However, deciding the truncation level at runtime can be achieved by using the above function to obtain multiple pointers to functions with different truncations and conditionally using the desired one.
%
\cref{lst:raptor_mem_usage}c shows how the mem-mode truncation can be used. Using mem-mode is more contrived and will be covered in detail in \cref{sec:mem-mode}.




