\subsection{Instrumentation Pass}
The \tool{} pass is implemented as an instrumentation pass on the LLVM intermediate representation (or LLVM IR)~\cite{lattner_llvm_2004}. LLVM is used by the clang (C/C++) and flang compilers among others. This allows us to handle all of Fortran, C, and C++ under a common representation suitable for compiler transformation and analysis\footnote{We are providing examples in C++, however \tool{}'s concepts apply equivalently to C and Fortran as well (with syntactical differences).}. We build on top of Enzyme's~\cite{moses_instead_2020} infrastructure and make use of its instrumentation and plugin utilities\footnote{Enzyme is an auto-differentiation framework for LLVM.}.

%\subsubsection{Transformation}
\paragraph{Transformation}
\cref{lst:raptor_usage_and_transformation}a shows an example of the transformation when the user has requested a function scope truncation.
It is shown in C++ for illustration purposes, however, the actual code being transformed is in LLVM IR.
First, the pass finds all functions that the user requested be truncated and the truncation configuration (in this case, the user requested the function \texttt{foo} to be truncated). 
Then, we find all transitively called functions (in this case, \texttt{foo} and \texttt{bar}), and for each of them, we %find all 
replace all operations on \fp{} numbers 
%and replace them 
with calls into our runtime.
Here, by utilizing features available in LLVM, we can recognize \fp{} arithmetic and functions in math libraries, such as the standard C and C++ math library.
All affected functions are cloned transformations in order to preserve the behavior of unrelated code that uses them.
%
When the requested scope is file- or program-level, our pass applies the same transformation to the \flops{} to all functions, without the special handling required for function-scope truncation.
%
This pass is implemented as an LLVM pass plugin, 
%This 
allowing it to be used in compilers such as clang or flang via their plugin interface in supported linkers (lld and gold), and as a standalone pass.
%, or additional compiler pass, loadable via the LLVM plugin interface, and the target precision is controlled at compile time.

\paragraph{Preserving Compiler Optimizations}
The stage of the optimization pipeline in which the pass runs dictates how closely the truncated operations match the optimized version of the \flop{}. 
Compilers have various optimizations that affect \fp{} computation, e.g., vectorization or fusion.
However, they can only handle operations they are aware of.
%For example, in \cref{lst:raptor_pass_op_mode_before}a, the code contains a \texttt{sqrt} call to the libc math library, and \fp{} arithmetic, which are known to the optimizer and it can treat them appropriately.
For example, in \cref{lst:raptor_pass_op_mode_before}a, the code contains \fp{} arithmetic and a \texttt{sqrt} call to the libc math library, both of which are known to the optimizer, so it can treat them appropriately.
However, once the code has been transformed (\cref{lst:raptor_usage_and_transformation}a) into calls into our runtime, the optimizer no longer understands the semantics of the operation and no further \flop{} optimization is possible.

Ideally, our pass would run after generic \fp{} optimizations are done to preserve as many compiler optimizations as possible. However, due to current limitations (see \cref{sec:limits})\done{enzyme? just not enough time to implement? can we get details?}, we are not able to handle vectorized instructions, which means we opt to run our pass before the vectorization passes.

\paragraph{Linking}
If a call to a function with no definition is encountered while attempting to truncate, we are not able to instrument any operations inside it.
For this reason, we configure the compiler(s) to use link-time optimizations (LTO), which merges all LLVM modules %that come 
from different files into one file.
We run our pass after the merge, which allows us to analyze the entire call graph\footnote{Calls to pre-compiled external libraries will be ignored and we emit a warning.}, see \cref{fig:raptor-overview}.

%However, this still prevents us from handling any function which is in a pre-compiled external library and only linked in at the final binary stage of the compilation. This is a limitation of our approach, however, it can be circumvented by also including the external library in the compilation. To assist the user in finding problems like this, our pass emits warnings when unknown external functions are detected in a truncation region.\todo{mitigations belong into limitations section; it already has some info on external libs; please double check and make description of true behavior consistent between here and the limitations section}


