%\noindent{\bf Incompressible multiphase fluid dynamics} 
The application in the incompressible regime is a rising bubble benchmark (\app{Bubble}). This solver employs a fractional-step projection method to evolve the velocity field and a sharp-interface ghost fluid method to model multiphase interfacial dynamics \cite{Dhruv_2024,Dhruv2020}.
%As a baseline validation,
%We consider a two-dimensional rising bubble benchmark problem . 
Here, a circular air bubble of diameter $d=1.0$ is initialized with its center at the origin, $(0, 0)$, within a two-dimensional rectangular domain. The air–water interface is tracked using a level-set function, $\phi$, where $\phi > 0$ denotes the air phase, $\phi < 0$ corresponds to the water phase, and $\phi = 0$ defines the air-water interface.

This \app{Bubble} simulation is governed by key dimensionless parameters for fluid and flow configuration. With $\rho'=1000$ and $\mu'=100$, we denote the density and viscosity ratios between water and air, respectively. These two parameters control the scaling of the fluid forces between the two phases. Furthermore, the Reynolds number ($\text{Re}$), Froude number ($\text{Fr}$), and Weber number ($\text{We}$) characterize the relative importance of inertial, gravitational, and surface tension forces. These numbers are set for water phase and scaled in air by the density and viscosity ratios, and we use $\text{Fr} = 1$ and $\text{We} = 125$. This configuration corresponds to a benchmark case reported in~\cite{Dhruv_2024,dhruv_lab-notebooksoutflow-forcing-revised_2023}.

%To investigate the effects of operator truncation on bubble deformation and breakup, we first simulate the problem up to time $t=3$ without applying any truncation, focusing on the case with $\text{Re} = 35$. 
%The computational mesh is adaptively managed using the AMReX library~\cite{AMReX_JOSS}, which also provides the multigrid solver needed for the pressure Poisson equation arising in the projection step.
We use the solution at $t=3$ computed at $\text{Re} = 35$ as the starting point for a series of simulations in which truncation is applied to both advection and diffusion operators of the Navier-Stokes solver. The advection terms are discretized using a fifth-order Weighted Essentially Non-Oscillatory (WENO) scheme, while a second-order central difference scheme is used for diffusion. These simulations are performed at $\text{Re} = 3500$. The choice of higher $\text{Re}$ for truncation tests was motivated by the need to accelerate bubble splitting and deformation in a shorter time period from $t=3$ to $t=4$. Our working hypothesis for this \app{Bubble} test is as follows:
%, allowing us to assess how truncation influences bubble breakup and deformation.
\ourHypothesis{3}{\label{hyp3}The required precision will align with the AMR refinement strategy, specifically that reducing precision in lower-resolution blocks will not significantly degrade the overall quality of the simulation. We also expect that numerical precision will influence the evolution of the bubble interface, affecting deformation, splitting, and shape over time. Since the precision in velocity calculations directly impacts bubble dynamics, we expect noticeable differences as the precision varies.} %\todo{Second part of the hypothesis is the most important and needs to go in front. Goal of our hypotheses is to reason and predict whether truncating in a certain way (e.g. dynamically based on AMR) will improve the error compared to just truncating everything. Our goal is not to predict how the simulation will react to truncation.}\todo{we need 5 of these boxes; 1 for each studies case}
