\subsection{Current Limitations}\label{sec:limits}
For our proof-of-concept, we omitted a few quality-of-life features.
For example, not all elementary functions are implemented, but adding additional functions is trivial if MPFR already supports them, otherwise minor engineering is required.
%\tool{} does not truncate regions which include calls to external, pre-compiled libraries within the region's call stack.
%\footnote{Now, we avoid it by compiling the library itself with \tool{} and LLVM LTO mode.}.
%This can partially be fixed by improving op-/mem-modes to switch back to the original precision before the external call.
\tool{} does not truncate function calls to external, pre-compiled libraries\footnote{This can
%partially 
be mitigated by making the function definitions available, either by providing source code or static libraries compiled with link-time optimization (LTO).} %LTO\footnote{Link-time optimization}.
or calls to dynamic function pointers.
We have not tested OpenMP support or thread-safety in general, original \fp{} operands have to be FP32 or FP64, and we do not support handwritten vector assembly. These limitations can be overcome with small engineering effort.\todo{Ivan: overcoming the handwritten assembly limitation is not low effort and I don't believe is a valid strategy, TODO think of better phrasing}
As shown in \cref{lst:usage}, the user has to manually change a few lines of code. To improve the user experience, \tool{} can be enhanced for easier region annotation (e.g. \verb|#pragma trunc...|) and to support function filtering using a configuration file (similar to profilers).
Programming languages other than C/C++ and Fortran could be supported if they lower to LLVM IR.
Lastly, our truncation parameters are compile-time constants, but deciding the truncation level at runtime can be achieved by compiling multiple function pointers for different truncations and conditionally using them.

