We seek a generic theory for adaptive dynamics.  We consider the dynamics of a system, like a biological organism, that has some \textit{fitness} based on its ability to track dynamical change in its environment.  The ability to adapt to varying environments is fundamental for biological survival and success \cite{murugan_roadmap_2021,levien_non-genetic_2021,bernhardt_life_2020}, prompting extensive theoretical \cite{kussell_phenotypic_2005,wolf_diversity_2005,donaldson-matasci_when_2013,belete_optimality_2015,xue_environment--phenotype_2019} and experimental \cite{acar_stochastic_2008,grantham_case_2016,gremer_within-and_2016,maxwell_when_2017} investigation into organisms' adaptive strategies. These strategies can be broadly classified into two types: (1) tracking strategies, where the organism senses the environment and shifts its phenotype or internal state according to the current \cite{lambert_memory_2014,nevozhay_mapping_2012} or anticipated \cite{saigusa_amoebae_2008,tagkopoulos_predictive_2008,mitchell_adaptive_2009,whitehead_diurnally_2009,marzen_optimized_2018,jabbur_bacteria_2024} conditions, and (2) non-tracking strategies (which we call ``generalism'' in the broad sense), including the ``pure'' generalist  (a single robust phenotype that does not switch) \cite{wang_evolving_2019,sachdeva_tuning_2020}  and stochastic bet-hedging  (random, environment-independent switching between phenotypes) \cite{slatkin_hedging_1974,venable_bet_2007,grimbergen_microbial_2015,muller_bet-hedging_2013,solopova_bet-hedging_2014}. Previous work has analyzed and computed optimal strategies for given environmental scenarios \cite{kussell_phenotypic_2005,mayer_diversity_2016,hufton_intrinsic_2016,hufton_phenotypic_2018,dinis_pareto-optimal_2022} and revealed (phase) transitions to distinct optimal states in different environmental settings \cite{mayer_transitions_2017,skanata_evolutionary_2016,patra_emergence_2015,xue_environment--phenotype_2019}. However, identifying the optimal strategy alone offers rather limited guidance on the underlying mechanisms and how a suboptimal organism can adjust to improve long-term growth. Knowing where a summit is does not inherently reveal the trail to reach it. We present a general theory of adaptivity which can compute optimized situations and routes to achieve them.\\

To address this gap, we here derive a useful relationship between the long-term growth rate (population fitness) of an adaptive population, the dynamics of its environment, and the population's strategy. A recent framework of nonequilibrium statistical physics \cite{yang_deriving_2024,yang_nonequilibrium_2024} gives a rigorous way to decompose fitness into two distinct components: a baseline non-tracking term quantifying the level of generalism, plus the gain from tracking the environment: \begin{equation}
    \text{fitness} = \text{generalism} +\text{tracking}.
\end{equation}
Here, we give quantitative applications that show how organisms can optimize their matching to environmental dynamics.  We give two examples of a system adapting to match an environment's dynamics: stochastic phenotype switching rates \cite{kussell_phenotypic_2005,belete_optimality_2015} and phenotypic memory \cite{skanata_evolutionary_2016}. And for the opposite task of controlling an environment to affect the fitness of a given a system -- for example in drug delivery strategies to kill potentially drug-resistant pathogens -- we compute optimized stochastic or periodic one-drug on-off switching. \\

Our decomposition of fitness aligns with the general equilibrium-nonequilibrium decompositions widely used in stochastic thermodynamics \cite{seifert_entropy_2005, wang_potential_2008, esposito_three_2010,ge_physical_2010,yang_potentials_2021,yang_time-translational_2023}. It shows explicitly that tracking necessarily involves nonequilibrium (NEQ) probability fluxes, clarifying the fundamental connection between time irreversibility and adaptive dynamics \cite{mustonen_fitness_2010,zhang_potential_2012,qian_fitness_2014,kobayashi_fluctuation_2015,genthon_fluctuation_2020, rao_evolutionary_2022}. In our examples below, these NEQ probability fluxes can arise from responsive phenotype switching, the combined effect of natural selection and bet-hedging, and/or anticipatory phenotype switching.\\

\textbf{How well does a system synchronize with its environment?}
First, we derive a basic relationship for how well a system is able to follow changes in its environment. Say we have any general system that takes on different states $\{x\}$ sitting in an environment that takes on different states $\{E\}$. We have a combined space $(E,x)$ of allowed environment-system states. The joint probability of finding the system in state $x$ and the environment in state $E$---or equivalently, of finding the combined environment-system in state $\{(E,x)\}$---is $\pi_{E,x}$.  This joint probability describes the long-term time fraction of the joint state $(E,x)$ if a steady-state exists.\footnote{The steady state has been called the ``red queen'' scenario \cite{van_valen_new_1973,ao_laws_2005,zhang_potential_2012,qian_fitness_2014}, a situation where a species constantly adapts to a changing environment.  This term comes from Alice in Wonderland which says that you must always keep running just to ``stay in place'' (stay alive).} %\textcolor{red}{something like this?} 
\\

Given the joint distribution, we can compute the marginal distributions ($\pi_E=\sum_x \pi_{E,x}$ and $\pi_x=\sum_E \pi_{E,x}$) and the conditional distribution ($\pi_{x|E}=\pi_{E,x}/\pi_E$) to derive a general \textit{parsing} to the joint probability $\pi_{E,x}$ as a measure of system-environment \textit{synchrony}. See Appendix A of the Supplementary Material (SM) \cite{SI} for derivation: 
\begin{equation} \label{eq: STR}
    \pi_{E,x}=\pi_E \, \pi_x+ \sum_{E' (\neq E)} \pi_E \, \pi_{E'} \, (\pi_{x|E}-\pi_{x|E'}).
\end{equation} 
The first term on the right represents a baseline that can be matched with a system independent of the environment (generalism) whereas the second term represents how much a state $x$ is preferred in environment $E$ opposed to other environments $E'$ (tracking). This latter term is the additional contribution from system-environment coupling. To see this, consider a simple two-state sensor aiming to synchronize its state $x=(a,b)$ with a two-state environment $E=(A,B)$, Eq. \eqref{eq: STR} tells us that concentrated conditional distributions ($\pi_{a|A}>\pi_{a|B}$ and $\pi_{b|B}>\pi_{b|A}$) are needed for the sensor to outperform an independent oscillator (that is, to have $\pi_{A,a}>\pi_A~\pi_a$ and $\pi_{B,b}>\pi_B~\pi_b$).\\

This expresses how tracking affects synchrony. This synchrony parsing is valid for any adaptive dynamics that reaches a joint dynamical steady-state under a varying environment.  Below, we now show that sustaining such nonzero conditional distribution differences requires nonequilibrium probability fluxes. \\


\textbf{To track its environment, a system requires nonequilibrium fluxes.}
To relate tracking to nonequilibrium flux,
we suppose that the combined environment-system follows a continuous-time Markov jump process. In each infinitesimal time step $\text{d}t$, only two types of Markov transitions are possible: either the system changes $(E,x)\mapsto(E,x')$ with rates $R_{xx'}(E)$ or the environment changes $(E,x)\mapsto(E',x)$ with rates $R_{EE'}(x)$. A simultaneous change of both $E$ and $x$ is higher order, $\mathcal{O}(\text{d}t^2)$, which is negligible in continuous time.  This is known as a \textit{bipartite} process \cite{hartich_stochastic_2014,horowitz_thermodynamics_2014,leighton_information_2024}. At steady state, the total outward net flux from any joint state $(E,x)$ must be zero: \begin{align}
    0 =& \sum_{E'(\neq E)} \pi_{E,x} R_{EE'}(x) - \pi_{E',x}R_{E'E}(x) \nonumber\\
    &+\sum_{x'(\neq x)} \pi_{E,x} R_{xx'}(E) -\pi_{E,x'}R_{x'x}(E).
\end{align}
If we further assume that the \textit{environment evolves on its own},\footnote{This is approximately true if the environment is controlled by an agent external to (and using no knowledge from) the system, if the environment is much larger than the system, or if the system feedback is much slower.} \textit{i.e.} all environment transition rates $R_{EE'}(x)=R_{EE'}$ are independent of the system's state, it can then be shown that, in order to get $\pi_{E,x}\neq \pi_E \pi_x$, we must have  nonzero system-wise net fluxes (See Appendix B of SM \cite{SI} for derivation and discussion): \begin{subequations}
    \begin{align} J_{xx'}(E)&=\pi_{E,x} R_{xx'}(E) -\pi_{E,x'}R_{x'x}(E) \\ &\neq 0, \text{ for some }(x,x',E).
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
%\textcolor{red}{Guys -- See if you can find more informative evocative terms than SFR and STR, plain english please.}  
In other words, the \textit{synchrony parsing} in Eq. \eqref{eq: STR} is related to \textit{nonequilibrium fluxes} as follows:\footnote{We are not able to derive a generic expression for the second term in Eq. \eqref{eq: synchrony flux relation}. But we have shown that this second term must be zero if all $J_{xx';E}=0$, regardless of if the environment is equilibrium or not (i.e. $J_{EE'}=0$ or not). This second term is linear to the fluxes $J_{xx'}(E)$ divided by the environmental switching rates $R_{EE}$, in our examples.}\begin{equation} \label{eq: synchrony flux relation}
    \pi_{E,x}=\pi_E\, \pi_x+[~\text{gain from } J_{xx'}(E)~].
\end{equation} 
The system's nonequilibrium net fluxes are needed for an adaptive system's synchrony to be higher than an independent (non-adaptive) system's would be.\footnote{See Appendix C of SM \cite{SI} for how the system's net fluxes can be further decomposed into cycle fluxes within the system and those involving both system and environment. The former can be understood as the system's intrinsic nonequilibriumness. Appendix C shows that both types of cycle fluxes can contribute to tracking.} This is a main result of this paper. The synchrony $\pi_{E,x}$ can be further mapped to the fitness as the long-term growth rate, provided the instantaneous growth rates $g_{x}(E)$ of system $x$ under environment $E$ are known.  The examples below illustrate what insights result from decomposing the adaptive system's fitness into generalist and tracking components. \\

\section*{Example 1: Adapting to a 2-State Markov Switching Environment} 
Here, we illustrate how system-environment dynamics parses into generalist and tracking components in a two-state system.  Suppose the system can take either of two states $(a,b)$ under a two-state switching environment $(A,B)$. State $a$ is more fit in environment $A$, and state $b$ is more fit in $B$. For a Markov switching environment and system, the joint system-environment state space is a square with four nodes and four edges, as illustrated in Fig. \ref{fig: square}(a).  This model can describe, for example, a single chemo-sensing receptor in \textit{E coli} in the fast (un)binding limit \cite{barato_efficiency_2014}, or---our focus here---the slow growth limit of phenotype-switching individuals in a population. The synchrony parsing in Eq.~\eqref{eq: STR} now becomes \begin{subequations} \label{eqs: STRs for the first example}
\begin{align}
    \pi_{Aa} = \pi_A \, \pi_a &+ \pi_A \pi_B (\pi_{a|A}-\pi_{a|B}) \\
    \pi_{Ab} = \pi_A \, \pi_b &- \pi_A \pi_B (\pi_{b|B}-\pi_{b|A})\\
    \pi_{Ba} = \pi_B \, \pi_a &- \pi_A \pi_B (\pi_{a|A}-\pi_{a|B})\\
    \pi_{Bb} = \pi_B \, \pi_b &+ \pi_A \pi_B (\pi_{b|B}-\pi_{b|A}).
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
Let the growth rate of $x$ under $E$ be $g_{x}(E)$; assume it is much slower than all switching rates ($R_{xx';E}$ or $R_{EE'}$) so that, for this example only, the population fractions are dominated by the switching and are approximately not impacted by the growth terms. The {Long-Term per capita Growth Rate (LTGR)} of the phenotype-switching population $N(t)$ can then be {parsed} as followed:
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
    \text{LTGR}&=\lim_{t\rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{t} \ln \frac{N(t)}{N(0)}\approx\sum_{Ex} \pi_{Ex} ~g_{x}(E)\\
    &= \underset{\text{generalism}}{\underbrace{\pi_a\bar{g}_a+ \pi_b \bar{g}_b}} + \underset{\text{tracking}}{\underbrace{\pi_A \pi_B \left[ \pi_{a|A} -\pi_{a|B}\right] \Delta_g}} \label{eq: fitness-tracking in example 1}
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
where $\bar{g}_x = \pi_A g_{x}(A)+\pi_B g_{x}(B)$ is the environment-averaged growth rate of type $x$ and $\Delta_g = [g_{a}(A)-g_{b}(A)]+[g_{b}(B)-g_{a}(B)]$ is the total growth rate difference between the more and less fit states.\\

In this example, the \textit{tracking} term is directly related to the \textit{cyclic net probability flux} in the  square joint state space. By using $J=\pi_{Ab} R_{AB}-\pi_{Ba} R_{BA}=\pi_{Ba} R_{BA}-\pi_{Aa}R_{AB}$ and denoting the average environment dwell time as $\bar{T}_A=1/R_{AB}$ and $\bar{T}_B=1/R_{BA}$, one can show that (See Appendix D of SM \cite{SI} for derivation)\begin{equation}\label{eqs: FTR for square}
        \pi_{a|A}-\pi_{a|B} = \pi_{b|B}-\pi_{b|A}= \bar{T}\cdot J
\end{equation} where $\bar{T}=\bar{T}_A+\bar{T}_B$ is the average environmental cycle period. This gives a distribution-flux parsing to the fitness: \begin{equation} \label{eq: LTGR decomposed in pi-J}
    \text{LTGR} = {\pi_a\bar{g}_a+ \pi_b \bar{g}_b} + \pi_A \pi_B~ (\bar{T}\cdot J) ~\Delta_g.
\end{equation}
This fitness parsing gives two implications on how environment, strategy, and fitness are related.\\


\begin{figure}
\begin{centering}
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{Fig1.png}
\par\end{centering}
\caption{\textbf{The state space, observables, and types of system-environment coupling of a simple adapting individual.} \textbf{(a)} The total state space of the two-state system $\{a,b\}$ under a Markov environmental switch $\{A,B\}$. Vertical arrows are environmental transitions and horizontal arrows are system transitions. \textbf{(b)} Four statistical observables that fully parameterize the system's dynamics. $\pi_a$ is the long-term time fraction that the system is in $a$ regardless of the environmental state. Traffic $\tau_E$ are the sum of fluxes in both direction under environment $E$. $J$ is the net cycle flux. \textbf{(c)} Three types of system-environmental coupling classified by the observables. Net flux $J$ is needed to track the environment and outperform an independent switch in the synchronization task.\label{fig: square}}
\end{figure}


On the one hand, the marginal state distribution $\pi_a$ and cycle flux $J$ are two dynamical features that quantify statistically the levels of ``generalism'' and ``tracking'', respectively, in the system. Pure generalism corresponds to $\pi_a=0$ or 1, and strong tracking is represented by a large flux $J$. Together with two other symmetric flux observables (traffic), $\tau_E = \pi_{Ea}R_{ab;E} + \pi_{Eb} R_{ba;E}$ where $E=A,B$, these four observables $(\pi_a,\tau_A,\tau_B,J)$ uniquely parameterize the four dimensions of the system's dynamics (Fig. \ref{fig: square}b), which are the system's four transition rates $R_{xx';E} = (\tau_E \pm J)/(2\pi_{Ex})$---where $\pi_{Ex}$ is determined by $\pi_a$ and $J$ by  Eqs. \eqref{eqs: STRs for the first example} and Eq. \eqref{eqs: FTR for square}. This shows that the system has three equilibrium dimensions $(\pi_a,\tau_A,\tau_B)$ and one nonequilibrium dimension parameterized by $J$, and only $\pi_a$ and $J$ affect the fitness directly.\footnote{The traffic terms can affect the fitness indirectly. The two traffic terms are the upper bound the net flux, $J\le \min(\tau_A,\tau_B)$, by their definitions. Thus, raising the traffic terms could give ``more room'' for the tracking flux to increase the fitness.}  The difference $|\tau_A-\tau_B|$ can represent a symmetric coupling between the environment and the system, but such a coupling has no direct contribution to the fitness:\footnote{Note that if a system has symmetric coupling to the environment, defined as having asymmetric traffic
$\tau_A\neq \tau_B$ but zero net flux $J=0$, then the system and the environment are statistically independent $\pi_{Ex}=\pi_E \pi_x$ but dynamically coupled, since in general $R_{xx';E}=\tau_E/(2\pi_{Ex})\neq R_{xx';E'}=\tau_{E'}/(2\pi_{E'x})$. Statics and dynamics are fundamentally different, and zero mutual information does not imply no coupling.} asymmetric, nonequilibrium coupling through $J$ is needed for a system to outperform an independent oscillator, as illustrated in Fig. \ref{fig: square}(c).\\

On the other hand, the fitness parsing in Eq. \eqref{eq: LTGR decomposed in pi-J} summarizes how environmental statistics or the growth rate profile affects the fitness. We make three points here. (1) If the fitter states $(Aa,Bb)$ are not much fitter, i.e. $\Delta_g$ is small, then the fitness gain from tracking is small as expected. Eq. \eqref{eq: LTGR decomposed in pi-J} quantifies that intuition. (2) If the environment switches must faster than the system, i.e. $1/\bar{T} \gg J$, then the fitness gain from tracking diminishes. Faster environments are harder to track, and they switch before you can build up significant benefit. (3) If the environment has a strong bias to one of its states, say $A$, then $\pi_A\rightarrow 0\Rightarrow\pi_A \pi_B \rightarrow 0$ and the gain from tracking also diminishes ($\bar{T}$ fixed). Tuning $\pi_a$ is more effective in this case.  This example illustrates some implications of parsing the fitness into generalism and tracking components.


\section*{Example 2: Achieving optimal Bet-Hedging}

We now go beyond the slow-growth limit of a population in Example 1 to consider a large phenotype-switching population where individuals can reproduce, die, or switch phenotype under a Markov switching environment, as illustrated in Fig. \ref{fig: evo example}(a). The same set of equations could also describe a species that reproduces with mutation (see Appendix E of SM \cite{SI}).  For simplicity, we assume the reproduction, death, and switching are Poissonian. Then, for a large population under a fixed environment $E$, the dynamics are described by ODEs: 
\begin{subequations} \label{eqs: ODEs population}
\begin{align}
\frac{\text{d}N_{a}}{\text{d}t} & =g_{a} (E)N_{a}-w_{ab}(E) N_{a}+w_{ba}(E) N_{b} \; , \\
\frac{\text{d}N_{b}}{\text{d}t} & =g_{b}(E)N_{b}+w_{ab}(E) N_{a}-w_{ba}(E) N_{b}, \; 
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
where $g_x=r_x-d_x$ is the net growth rate of type $x$ with reproduction rate $r_x$ and death rate $d_x$, which we presume to be constant and independent of the population size for simplicity.\\

\begin{figure}
\begin{centering}
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{Fig2.png}
\par\end{centering}
\caption{\textbf{The dynamics and total state space for a phenotype-switching population.} \textbf{(a)} We consider the population dynamics of two-state individuals with Poisson birth-death and switching. \textbf{(b)} The population type fraction (of $a$) tracks the environmental changes and determines the instantaneous per capita growth rate. \textbf{(c)} The system-environment total state space consists of two line segments $f\in [0,1]$, one for each environment $A$ and $B$. The population can jump between two segments at any $f$. \textbf{(d)} The (cyclic) net flux over the total state space leads to the long-term conditional probability density $\rho(f|E)$, forming a Flux-Tracking Relation for population dynamics.
\label{fig: evo example}}
\end{figure}

These equations are commonly used to model phenotypic switching in a population of microbes like \textit{E. coli} \cite{kussell_phenotypic_2005,skanata_evolutionary_2016} or yeast \cite{belete_optimality_2015}; it is standard to rewrite them in terms of more informative variables.
Denoting the total population $N=N_a+N_b$ and the population fraction of the $a$ phenotype $f = N_a/N$, these ODEs become
\begin{subequations} \label{eqs: ODEs population in N-f}
\begin{align}
\frac{\text{d} f}{\text{d}t} & = f(1-f)~\Delta_{ab}  +(f_w-f) \sigma_w \label{eq: ODE f}\; , \\
\frac{\text{d}N}{\text{d}t} & = [g_a f + g_b (1-f)]~N \label{eq: ODE N}\; .
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
where $\sigma_w = w_{ab}+w_{ba}$ is the sum of the switching rates, $f_w = w_{ba}/\sigma_w$ is the steady-state phenotype fraction if there's no growth (or death), and $\Delta_{ab} = g_a - g_b$ is the growth rate difference, also known as the selection coefficient. All these parameters depend on the environment.
The rewriting shows that the diversification mechanisms of phenotypic switching drive the type fraction $f$ toward $f_w$ with rate $\sigma_w$ whereas natural selection, via the other term of $\text{d}f/\text{d}t$,
%which takes the usual form \cite{kimura1969natural}, 
aims to fixate $f$ toward 0 or 1 with a rate $|\Delta_{ab}|$. The overall dynamics is a balance of the two. Most importantly, it shows that the type fraction $f$ is the sensing dimension that tracks the environmental changes, whereas the $N$ dynamics in Eq. \eqref{eq: ODE N} converts $f$ into the per capita growth rate as the population's average fitness. See Fig. \ref{fig: evo example}(b) for an example trajectory. \\


\textbf{The phenotype fraction determines the Long-Term Growth Rate.}
We now derive the fitness parsing for this adaptive population.  The LTGR of the total population under a switching environment can be directly related to the long-term average $f$ conditioned on different environments $\mathcal{E}$:\begin{subequations}
    \begin{align}
        \text{LTGR} &= \lim_{t\rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{t} \ln \frac{N(t)}{N(0)}\\
        &=\sum_{\mathcal{E}} \pi_\mathcal{E} \left[g_{a}(\mathcal{E}) \langle f|\mathcal{E} \rangle + g_{b}(\mathcal{E}) (1-\langle f|\mathcal{E} \rangle)\right] \label{eq: LTGR in terms of conditional average f}
    \end{align}
\end{subequations}
where $\pi_\mathcal{E}$ is the long-term time fraction of environment $\mathcal{E}$ and \begin{equation}
    \langle f|\mathcal{E} \rangle = \lim_{t\rightarrow \infty} \frac{\int_0^\infty f(t) \delta_{E(t),\mathcal{E}} \text{d}t}{\text{total time in }\mathcal{E}} 
\end{equation} 
is the conditional long-term empirical average of $f$ under all epochs in environment $\mathcal{E}$. The term $\delta_{x,y}$ within the integral is a Kronecker delta function, which collapses the integral so that it is only taken over times when the system is in environment $\mathcal{E}$. For Markov switching environments, the conditional average $\langle f |\mathcal{E}\rangle$ can be expressed analytically and computed numerically without ensemble simulations \cite{hufton_intrinsic_2016,hufton_phenotypic_2018,dinis_pareto-optimal_2022}, which we've used throughout the paper.    \\

We can thus parameterize the system's state space with only the type fraction $f$.\footnote{This simplification is due to the density independence of the switching and growth rates. For more general population dynamics, such as the case with a finite carrying capacity, the $f$ dynamics will depend on $N$, and we will need both $f$ and $N$ to parameterize the system's state under the varying environment.} With a Markov switching environment $A\leftrightarrow B$, the system-environment joint state space is then composed of two line segments $f\in [0,1]$, one under each environment $A$ and $B$, as shown in Fig. \ref{fig: evo example}(c). \\

By computing the long-term average value of $f$, \begin{equation}
    \langle f \rangle = \lim_{t\rightarrow \infty} \frac{\int_0^\infty f(t)  \text{d}t}{\text{total time}} =\pi_A \langle f|A \rangle + \pi_B \langle f|B\rangle, 
\end{equation}
we get the fitness decomposition of this example by rewriting Eq. \eqref{eq: LTGR in terms of conditional average f}, which is a direct population generalization of the small growth case in Eq. \eqref{eq: fitness-tracking in example 1} with individual probability $\pi_a$ replaced by average population fraction $\langle f\rangle$: 
\begin{align}
    \text{LTGR} = &\langle f \rangle \bar{g}_a + \langle 1-f\rangle \bar{g}_b \nonumber\\
    &+ \pi_A \pi_B \left[ \langle f|A \rangle - \langle f|B \rangle\right] \Delta _g \label{eq: STR for LTGR}.
\end{align}
Further denote that $J(f,A)$ is the probability flux in the $f$ direction under environment $A$, as illustrated in horizontal, blue arrows in Fig. \ref{fig: evo example} (d), and $\bar{J}=\int_0^1 J(f,A) \text{d}f$ is the total net flux. Then, we can relate the tracking term in Eq. \eqref{eq: STR for LTGR} to net flux and get a fitness decomposition in the\textit{ exact same form} as our earlier case in Eq. \eqref{eq: LTGR decomposed in pi-J} (see Appendix F in SM \cite{SI} for derivation): \begin{equation} \label{SFR for LTGR in random environment}
     \text{LTGR} = { \langle f \rangle \bar{g}_a+ \langle 1-f \rangle \bar{g}_b} + \pi_A \pi_B~ (\bar{T} \cdot  \bar{J})  ~\Delta_g.
\end{equation} 
This shows that the relationship among environment, strategy, and fitness in Example 1 also applies to adaptive populations in Example 2.\\

\textbf{An optimization principle for stochastic bet-hedging.}
We now discuss how the fitness parsing in Eq. \eqref{SFR for LTGR in random environment} helps explain non-monotonic trends upon altering the system parameters \cite{kussell_phenotypic_2005,acar_stochastic_2008,belete_optimality_2015,skanata_evolutionary_2016}.
Bet-hedging is a common and often useful strategy for biological systems to survive varying environments \cite{levien_non-genetic_2021}. In the context of a phenotype-switching population, it is often implemented mechanistically through phenotype switching with rates $w_{ab}$ and $w_{ba}$.  The population maintains diversity through switching, preventing natural selection from fixing either phenotype (reaching $f = 0$ or $f = 1$). Here, we discuss how such diversification helps the long-term survival of the population in the case where $w_{ab}$ and $w_{ba}$ are independent of the environment, often called \textit{stochastic switching} \cite{kussell_phenotypic_2005}.\\

Kussell and Leibler have shown that, in the fast growth limit where $g_x(E)\gg$ all $R_{EE'}$ and $w_{xx'}$, the optimal stochastic switching rate equals the environmental switching rate \cite{kussell_phenotypic_2005}, see Fig. \ref{fig: SR evolution}(a) for an illustration. When the environment is not slow, however, the existence and position of the optimal switching rate depend on both the environment and the growth rate profile \cite{belete_optimality_2015}. Our fitness decomposition explains why and gives a sufficient condition for the existence of optimal non-zero switching rates.\\

We've parsed fitness into two parts in Eq. \eqref{SFR for LTGR in random environment}: one associated with the long-term average baseline fraction $\langle f \rangle$ (first two terms) and another related to the net flux and environmental tracking (last term). First, we note that the tracking term is always bell-shape-like in the two dimensional space of stochastic switching rate $w$, always attaining a maximum on the interior of the region and never on the boundary. This is due to two distinct features. On the one hand, it vanishes when either of the stochastic switching rates is tiny---causing near fixation because of the selection force $\text{d}f/\text{d}t \approx  f(1-f)~\Delta_{ab}$. On the other hand, when switching rates ($w_{ab}$ and $w_{ba}$) are both large, it screens out the environment-dependent natural selection by $\text{d}f/\text{d}t \approx (f_w-f) \sigma$, also leading to vanishing flux $\bar{J}$. Therefore, the tracking contribution is always a bell shape, as illustrated on the right column throughout Fig. \ref{fig: SR evolution}(a-c).\\

