We model a periodically-switching environment as the deterministic limit of a switch with infinite intermediate Markov transitions as illustrated in Fig. \ref{fig: from stochastic to deterministic env}(a). The state space of the environment is a ring, and the environment-system joint state space is now two rings, as shown in gray in Fig. \ref{fig: from stochastic to deterministic env}(b). Since the parsing to marginal and conditional probabilities in Eqs. \eqref{eq: STR}, \eqref{eqs: STRs for the first example}, and \eqref{eq: STR for LTGR} are valid generally, no matter if the environmental switching is stochastic or deterministic, our task here is to derive how the NEQ probability flux relates to the conditional probability difference for periodic environments. The results, in comparison to the Markov random environment, are summarized in Fig. \ref{fig:table} and explained as follows.  \\

