\textbf{The value of anticipation depends on the phase of the environment.}
We first derive the fitness parsing in the pure switching case.
For a switch $a\leftrightarrow b$ under a periodic environment, let us define $\mathcal{J}(\theta)$ as the net flux density from $b$ to $a$ at a given phase of the environment $\theta \in{[0,T_A+T_B]}$, along the blue horizontal edges in Fig. \ref{fig: from stochastic to deterministic env}(b). Then,  %$\int_0^{T_A +T_B} \mathcal{J}(t)\text{d}t=0$ due to stationarity and probablity conservation., 
we can derive the following relation between net flux density and conditional probabilities (see Appendix G in SM \cite{SI} for derivation and discussion)
\begin{equation} \label{eq; SFR for switch sync with periodic switch}
    \pi_{a|A}-\pi_{a|B} = \mathcal{K} \circ \mathcal{J},   
\end{equation}
where the contributions of the NEQ flux at different phases are superimposed with a linear kernel $\mathcal{K}$:
\begin{subequations}\label{eq: kernal}
\begin{align} 
    \mathcal{K} \circ \mathcal{J} &= \int_0^{T_A} \left(\frac{1}{2 } - \frac{\theta}{T_A}\right) {\mathcal{J}(\theta)} \text{d} \theta  \\
     &+  \int_0^{T_B} \left(\frac{1}{2}-\frac{\theta}{T_B} \right) {[-\mathcal{J}(T_A+\theta)]} \text{d}\theta.
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
Similar to the Markov random environment case in Eq. \eqref{eqs: FTR for square}, this tracking term sums contributions from the two environments. The difference is that the phase matters under periodic environments, so the two terms $\bar{T}_A J + \bar{T}_B J$ in the Markov random case are replaced by integration over the flux profiles respectively.
Together with  Eq. \eqref{eqs: STRs for the first example}, this result shows the synchrony gain from fluxes for a two-state system in a periodic environment,\begin{equation} \label{eq: SFR for periodic environment}
    \pi_{Aa}-\pi_A \pi_a = \pi_{Bb}-\pi_B \pi_b = \pi_A \pi_B \  \mathcal{K}\circ \mathcal{J}
\end{equation}
This linear kernel $\mathcal{K}$  demonstrates the benefit of anticipatory fluxes in a periodic environment: for the latter half of each environmental epoch ($\theta>T_A/2$, $\theta > T_B/2$), anticipatory net fluxes toward the currently less fit state ($a \rightarrow b$ in $A$ and $b \rightarrow a$ in $B$) contribute positively. Furthermore, this gain from anticipatory flux gets higher when $\theta$ is closer to the switching time $T_A$ or $T_B$. 
%\textcolor{red}{CK: I think $\theta = t$, right? So we need to switch one.} 
A toy example is presented in Appendix H of SM \cite{SI} to show how late anticipatory flux helps the synchronization task and how anticipatory systems are more susceptible to switching errors. \\

There are, in fact, multiple mathematically-equivalent expressions of the kernel because the integration of net flux density $\mathcal{J}$ over the full cycle must be zero---there should be no net probability transport from $b$ to $a$ over  the whole period. To illustrate this mathematical degeneracy, consider the simplest flux density with two Dirac delta function pulses: $\mathcal{J}(\theta)= J \delta(\theta-\theta_1) - J \delta(\theta-\theta_2)$. The two pulses must have the same strength to be integrated to zero over the whole period. These pulses are anticipatory if $0\le \theta_2\le T_A$ and $T_A \le \theta_1 \le T_A+T_B$. We then get $\mathcal{K}\circ \mathcal{J}=[{\theta_2}/{T_A}-{1}/{2}] J+[{(\theta_1-T_A)}/{T_B}-{1}/{2}]J=[{\theta_2}/{T_A}+{(\theta_1-T_A)}/{T_B}-1]J.$ This shows that the ``1/2'' factors can be replaced with any two constants $p,q$ that summed to one, $p+q=1$: a mathematical degeneracy due to the two pulses having the same strength. However, physically, we expect the gain to be the same from employing the same type of anticipatory fluxes within the two environments. This ``fixes the gauge'' to the equal splitting $p=q=1/2$ for physical interpretations. For example, if both pulses are located at 2/3 of each environmental epoch, i.e. $\theta_2=2T_A/3$ and $\theta_1=T_A+2T_B/3$, then the gains from both fluxes should be the same: in this case, both $(2/3 - 1/2) J = J/6$. In short, Eq. \eqref{eq: SFR for periodic environment} gives the synchrony-flux relation under periodic environments with different kernels characterizing the benefits of well-timed anticipation. \\

\textbf{Kernel $\mathcal{K}$ captures the fitness gain of fluxes at different phases of the periodic environment.}  %\textcolor{red}{Same kernel as what?} 
%\textcolor{blue}{
The kernel $\mathcal{K}$ gives the fitness parsing for an adapting population with growth. For the adapting population, the system's state space is now a real line segment $[0,1]$ instead of just two states $(a,b)$. The system-environment joint state space then becomes the two-dimensional curved side of the cylinder as shown at the lower-right of Fig. \ref{fig:table}. We can then derive (see  Appendix I of SM \cite{SI}) 
\begin{equation}
    \langle f|A \rangle - \langle f|B \rangle =\mathcal{K}\circ \bar{\mathcal{J}} \; , 
\end{equation}
where
$\bar{\mathcal{J}}(\theta)=\int_0^1 \mathcal{J}(f,\theta)\text{d}f= \frac{\text{d} \hat{f}(\theta)}{\text{d}\theta}$
is the total flux across the system's state space at environmental phase $t$
and $\hat{f}(\theta)$ is the solution of the ODE of $f$ under a periodic environment. The fitness parsing can then be derived: \begin{equation}
    \text{LTGR} = \bar{g}_a \langle f \rangle+ \bar{g}_b \langle 1-f \rangle + \pi_A \pi_B \,(\mathcal{K}\circ \bar{\mathcal{J}} ) \Delta_g. \label{eq: STR for LTGR periodic}
\end{equation}
This shows that (1) anticipatory fluxes can help tracking when well-timed (in the latter half of the environmental phase) and (2) the fitness gain from tracking also diminishes when the environment switches fast\footnote{When $T\rightarrow 0$, the integral 
\begin{equation*}
    \pm\int_0^{T} (1/2 - \theta/{T}) {\mathcal{J}(\theta)} \text{d}\theta \sim \mp\mathcal{J}(0) \frac{1}{T} \int_0^{T} ~\theta~ \text{d}\theta \rightarrow 0.
\end{equation*}} ($T_A,T_B\rightarrow 0$)  or when the environment is strongly biased ($\pi_A\rightarrow0$ or 1 such that $\pi_A\pi_B\rightarrow0$), similar to the random environment case. 



\section*{Example 5: How to Control Environments to Manipulate Systems}

So far, we considered adaptive system degrees of freedom in unpredictable environments.  Here, we consider the reverse: the system is given, and we can control the environment. We illustrate this with a toy model of drug resistance.\\% Generalization to more realistic models is forthcoming [cite Charles and Corey, preprint or simply put a footnote like reference].}\\

When drugs are applied to killing populations of pathogens, such as bacteria, cancer, or viruses, the resulting selective pressure leads the organism to become drug-resistant.  A sufficiently strong treatment of a drug will drive the rise of a resistant mutant, and subsequent treatment will fail. Accordingly, drug resistance can be viewed as an evolutionary game that we drug-givers are playing against the disease pathogen \cite{stein2023stackelberg}. An important successful example is ``integrated pest management'' \cite{whelan_resistance_2020,gatenby2020integrating}, where the problem of pesticide resistance has been controlled through better treatment schedules and other strategies explicitly designed to fight resistance. We now show how a toy model of drug resistance, similar to the one in \cite{robertson-tessi_feasibility_2023}, can be decomposed into components, to ultimately improve evolution-inspired treatment schedules. \\


Consider a pathogen with drug-sensitive population $N_S$ and drug-resistant population $N_R$. We assume that both subpopulations grow at rate $g$ without drug and that the sensitive one reduces to $g-\delta$ when the drug is on. We also assume that that switching from sensitive to resistant only happens under drug, with rate $r$, and re-sensitization happens only when there's no drug, with rate $s$. For a large population, the dynamics can be described by ODEs: 
\begin{subequations}
    \begin{align}
        \dot{N}_S &= (g-\delta D)~N_S -r DN_S + s (1-D)N_R\\
        \dot{N}_R &= g ~N_R +r DN_S - s (1-D)N_R
    \end{align}
\end{subequations}where $D=1$ or $0$ represents drug on or off. As before, we then rewrite them in terms of the total population $N$ and the population fraction of drug-resistant cells $f$ for drug-on, \begin{subequations}
    \begin{align}
    \dot{N}&=[g-(1-f)~\delta] N;\\
    \dot{f}&= f (1-f)~\delta+(1-f)~r,
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
and drug off, 
    \begin{equation}
    \dot{N}=g~ N \text{ and }
    \dot{f}=- s ~f.
\end{equation}
Denoting $\pi_{\text{on}}$ as the time fraction of drug-on, Eqs. \eqref{eq: STR for LTGR} and \eqref{eq: STR for LTGR periodic} gives us the population long-term growth rate under Markov stochastic or periodic drug on/off switching:  \begin{equation}
    \text{LTGR} = [g-\langle 1-f \rangle~\pi_{\text{on}}~ \delta]+(\text{tracking}) \label{eq: LTGR eq for the killing drug}
\end{equation} where the tracking term depends on the switching environment being Markov random, $\pi_{\text{on}} (1-\pi_{\text{on}}) ~(\bar{T}\cdot \bar{J}) ~\delta$, or periodic, $\pi_{\text{on}} (1-\pi_{\text{on}}) ~(\mathcal{K}\circ\bar{\mathcal{J}})~ ~\delta$. We show below that the two different components---generalism and tracking---can be controlled by different environmental features.\\

\begin{figure}
\begin{centering}
\includegraphics[width= \columnwidth]{Fig7.png}
\par\end{centering}
\caption{\textbf{Fast environment switching reduces tracking gain whereas optimal environment time fraction reduces generalism gain.} \textbf{(a)} shows the total long-term population growth rate of drug sensitive or resistant cells under Markov random on/off drug switching. On the left, we vary the average cycle period $\bar{T}=\bar{T}_{\text{on}} + \bar{T}_{\text{off}}$ where $\bar{T}_{\text{on/off}}$ represents long-term average drug on/off intervals and fix the drug-on time fraction $\pi_{\text{on}}=\bar{T}_{\text{on}}/\bar{T}$. On the right, we vary $\pi_{\text{on}}$ and fix $\bar{T}$.   \textbf{(b)} is similar to (a) but under periodic environment: $T=T_{\text{on}}+T_{\text{off}}$ where $T_{\text{on/off}}$ is the fixed interval of on/off and $\pi_{\text{on}}=T_{\text{on}}/T$. Parameters in both (a) and (b) are $g=1,\delta=5,r=1,s=3$. See Appendix J of SM \cite{SI} for how these are chosen. The time unit is chosen to be 100 days, i.e. rate quantities are per 100 days.
\label{fig: drug resistance}}
\end{figure} 

On the one hand, when the drug on/off switching is fast, i.e. when the on-off cycle period is small, the tracking term diminishes as it has a prefactor of the cycle period. The long-term population growth rate is dominated by the generalism term. The plot on the left of Fig. \ref{fig: drug resistance} shows this for (a) Markov random environment and (b) periodic environment. Fast switching reduces the LTGR gain from tracking the environment, which originates from the growth rate/fitness difference of sensitive and resistant cells. 
{Our results here for the one drug case are similar to the recommendation of Goldie, Coldman, and Gudauskas \cite{goldie1982rationale,strobl2023treatment,perry2012perry}, that fast alternating non-cross-resistant chemotherapy every cycle in cancer should lead to the best response. Our parsing gives a new interpretation for how population re-sensitization to drug impacts the LTGR. The strategy of alternating drugs has been applied with notable successes \cite{glick1998mopp,grier2003addition} and failures \cite{bonadonna1995sequential,aisner1995combination,de1995cmf}. Of course, our Fig.~\ref{fig: drug resistance} has only been made with one set of parameters in a toy model with only one drug and two phenotypes. We expect future generalizations of our result (to different parameter values and more realistic multi-state models) to explain the mixed clinical trial results and predict which dosing strategy will be optimal for inhibiting any given population.} \\

On the other hand, there's an optimal drug on/off time fraction $\pi_{\text{on}}$ for killing the population as shown on the right of Fig. \ref{fig: drug resistance} (a) and (b). Eq. \eqref{eq: LTGR eq for the killing drug} shows that this optimality is due to the generalism. In the two limits of none or all drug-on, $\pi_{\text{on}}\rightarrow 0$ or 1, the tracking term approaches zero and the generalism term reaches its maximal value $g$ because $\langle f \rangle\rightarrow 1$ as $\pi_{\text{on}}=1$ and both types have growth rate $g$ when there's no drug $\pi_{\text{on}}=0$. For levels in between, $0<\pi_{\text{on}}<1$, both the tracking term and the $\langle 1-f\rangle \pi_{\text{on}} \delta$ term in the generalism term become positive, leading to the opposite bell shapes of the two components on the right of Fig. \ref{fig: drug resistance}. The optimal level of $\pi_{\text{on}}$ increases if re-sensitization rate $s$ increases or resistant rate $r$ decreases (see Appendix J of SM \cite{SI}). \\

This example shows that the two components of LTGR are governed by different environmental features, and that fitness parsing helps interpret known results. The fast drug on/off switching primarily reduces the tracking gain whereas the optimal drug-on time fraction primarily reduces the generalism gain. The optimal levels of drug switching speed and on-time fraction are due to two distinct adaptive strategies---tracking and generalism. 

\section*{Summary and Discussion}

%\textcolor{red}{Guys -- Reduce this first paragraph to a sentence or two, and see if you can condense the second paragraph too.}

%We decompose the long-term growth rate (fitness) of an adaptive population into two fundamentally different components---generalism and tracking, weighted by the dynamical statistics of the environment. Our parsing characterizes the relationship among fitness, environmental dynamics, and an organism's adaptive strategy; explains the existence and mechanisms of optimal strategies; and indicates how general suboptimal systems can  approach the optimum. We presented five illustrative examples about a single two-state switch or a population of them under both Markov-stochastic or periodic environments. From which, we identified four rules of thumbs for adaptation to varying environments. First, tracking needs nonequilibrium net fluxes, but they are not the only knob---the equilibrium baseline set by generalism matters too. Second, fast switching or a strongly biased environment diminishes the fitness gain from tracking. Third, anticipatory fluxes can increase the fitness under periodic environments but only when realized at right environmental phases. Fourth, for killing drug-resistant populations, fitness gain from tracking and generalism can be suppressed respectively by fast switching and optimal time fraction of environments. \\
Parsing fitness into generalism and tracking components reveals key principles of adaptive dynamics: it explains optimal strategies like bet-hedging and phenotypic memory; clarifies why tracking gain diminishes in fast or biased environments, but can be boosted by well-timed anticipation in coherent ones; and shows how pathogenic populations can be optimally controlled by using fast environmental switching and optimal time-fractioning to independently suppress tracking and generalism.\\

%To close, we remark two potential generalizations and one interesting connection. First, we have mainly focused on systems with two phenotypes under binary environments (three-state systems under three-state environments are briefly discussed in Appendix C in SM \cite{SI}). Generalizations to cases with more states can give new principles for adaptive strategies. Second, we have assumed that the system does not feedback to the environment. Generalizations are needed to account for resource dynamics or the system's active change of its surroundings (e.g. chemotaxis/migration or ``actions'' from Friston \textit{et. al.} \cite{friston_free-energy_2010,friston_active_2012}). Lastly, besides the nonequilibrium physics connection \cite{esposito_three_2010,ge_physical_2010,wang_potential_2008,yang_potentials_2021}, our parsing could also be conceptually related to neural coding theory where generalism and tracking corresponds to ``rate coding'' and ``spike-time coding'' \cite{mainen_reliability_1995}, about whether a sensory neuron (system) encodes external stimulus (environment) statistically with probability of firing or precisely with a single spike---and the neuron's choices turn out to relate to the stochasticity of the environment \cite{taillefumier_phase_2013}.\\
We note two potential generalizations: extending the framework to multi-state systems or environments (Appendix C in SM \cite{SI}) and incorporating system-environment feedback mechanisms from finite resource or the system's active changes of environment \cite{friston_free-energy_2010,friston_active_2012} like migration. We also note a conceptual parallel to neuroscience, where the distinction between generalism and tracking is analogous to that between rate and spike-time coding---a choice of strategy in sensory neurons that itself can depend on the stochasticity of the environment  \cite{mainen_reliability_1995,taillefumier_phase_2013}.


