%     \frac{7}{4}\left[\delta(\omega)-h(\omega)-\frac{\Gamma^{2\vert d\vert}_dg(\omega)}{\omega^2+4|\ln\Gamma_d|^2}\right]
%     \!; &\!\!\!\!\! d<0\\
%     \delta(\omega);& \!\!\!\!\! d=0\\
%     4\delta(\omega); &\!\!\!\!\!  d>0
%     \end{cases}\,,
%     \label{eq:FXY-nonlocal-omega}
% }
% where $h(\omega)$ and $g(\omega)$ in the result for $d<0$ are oscillatory functions of $\omega$ with decaying envelopes (explicit expressions relegated to the \hyperref[sec:endmatter]{End Matter}) arising from the fact that $F^{X\overline{Y}}(t)$ is defined only for $t>|d|$ for $d<0$, whereas the Lorentzian is the manifestation of the exponential decay in time of $F^{X\overline{Y}}(t)$.

We next consider the second setting mentioned above, where the subsystem $X$ and $Y$ are macroscopic subsystems. 
Since, in this case, the eigenstate correlations are related to the two-point dynamical correlations and OTOCs of operators which themselves have macroscopic support, we find it more natural to express the results in terms of the opMI.
The relation in Eq.~\ref{eq:opMI-FXY} and the fact that $|X|+|Y| = L-2(t+d)+1$, implies that it is appropriate to study the rescaled eigenstate correlation $\braket{F^{XY}(t)}/2^{L+2(t+d)-1}$ which is nothing but $\braket{e^{I_2^{XY}(U_t)}}$. 
The circuit diagrams and the resulting transfer matrix in Eq.~\ref{eq:FXYt-nonlocal-circs} show that this evaluates to
\eq{
\braket{e^{I_2^{XY}(U_t)}}=\begin{cases}
    1+\frac{3(|d|+1)}{\Lambda^{\vert d \vert}}\Lambda^{2t}+\cdots\,; & \!\!\!\! d\leq  0\\
    1\,; & \!\!\!\!d>0
    \end{cases}\,,
	\label{eq:FXYt-nonlocal-res}
}
where, as before, $\Lambda = [2-\cos(4J)]/3$ and the ellipsis denotes further subleading corrections.
The result implies that the opMI between $X$ and $Y$ is identically zero if they fall entirely outside the lightcone whereas inside the lightcone while the opMI is non-zero, it decays exponentially with $t$ at long times with a rate which is independent of $d$.
Another fallout of the above result is that along a given velocity ray, defined by $i_Y=i_X+vt$, the opMI is given by $I_2^{XY}(U_t)\sim e^{-\gamma(v)t}$ at long times where $\gamma(v)=|\ln\Lambda|(1+v/2)$ for $v\leq 2$ and $\gamma(v)\to\infty$ for $v>2$ indicating an explicit velocity dependence in the eigenstate correlation. 

