\subsection{Model} \label{ssec:sIS2}
Let $(\Omega,\Sigma,\mbP)$ be a probability space and let $X:\Omega\mapsto\mX \subseteq \Real^{d_x}$, $Z:\Omega\mapsto\mZ \subseteq \Real^{d_z}$ and $Y:\Omega\mapsto\mY \subseteq \Real^{d_y}$ denote three multidimensional real random variables (r.v.'s). The r.v. $X$ represents the $d_x$-dimensional state of interest (or state), which we aim to estimate, $Y$ represents some $d_y$-dimensional observed data and $Z$ is a $d_z$-dimensional nuisance variable needed to model the relationship between $X$ and $Y$.

The complete model can be specified as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item The prior probability law of the state of interest $X$ is denoted $\pi_0(\sd x)$ and we assume the ability to generate random samples from this distribution.
\item The nuisance variable $Z$ and the state $X$ are related through a Markov kernel $\kappa^{d_z}(x,\sd z):\mX \times \mB(\mZ)\mapsto [0,1]$, where $\mB(\mZ)$ denotes the Borel $\sigma$- algebra of subsets of $\mZ$.
The superscript $d_z$ indicates explicitly that there is a (possible) dependence on the dimension of the nuisance variables $Z$. 
We use a similar notation for different objects all through the paper.
\item Given $X=x$ and $Z=z$, the observation $Y$ can be characterised by a conditional pdf w.r.t. the Lebesgue measure, which we denote as $g^{d_z}(y\mid x,z)$. Again, we highlight the dependence on $d_z$ and we use the notation $g_y^{d_z}(x,z):=g^{d_z}(y \mid x,z)$.
\item The likelihood of a state value $X=x$ given an observation $Y=y$ for this model is
\beq
l_y^{d_z}(x) := \int g_y^{d_z}(x,z) \kappa^{d_z}(x,\sd z).
\label{eq_ly}
\eeq 
Note, however, that the integral above cannot be computed exactly for most models of interest.
\end{itemize}
Our aim is to approximate the posterior law of the state $X$ conditional on the observation $Y=y$, denoted $\pi(\sd x)$, using IS. This posterior probability measure can be written as
\beq
\pi(\sd x) = \frac{
	l_y^{d_z}(x) \pi_0(\sd x)
}{
	\pi_0(l_y^{d_z})
}
\nn
\eeq
where the likelihood $l_y^{d_z}(x)$ is given by Eq. \eqref{eq_ly} and 
\beq 
\pi_0(l_y^{d_z}):=\int l_y^{d_z}(x) \pi_0(\sd x)
\nn
\eeq 
is the normalization constant of the distribution, for $y\in \mY$ fixed, and also corresponds to the marginal density of the r.v. \( Y \). We remark that both $l_y^{d_z}(x)$ and $\pi_0(l_y^{d_z})$ are analytically intractable in general with the exception of linear Gaussian models, discussed in Section \ref{sec:Linear_Gaussian}. 

\noindent Let us define the mixture measure \(\mix^{d_z}\) on \( \mB(\mX \times \mZ)\) (see \cite{baccelli2024random})  constructed as

\begin{equation}\label{eq:Mix_meas}
\mix^{d_z}( \sd (x, z)) :=  \kappa^{d_z}(x, \sd z) \, \pi_0(\sd x).
\end{equation}
This probability measure represents the joint distribution of the pair \((X, Z)\), where \(X \sim \pi_0\) and \(Z \sim \kappa^{dz}(x, \cdot)\). We assume that $g_y^{d_z}(x,z) \in L^2(\mix^{d_z}),$ $\forall \; y \in \mY$, i.e.,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:normL2}
\| g_y \|^2_{L^2(\mix^{d_z})}:=\int_{\mathcal{X}} \int_{\mathcal{Z}} g_y^2(x,z) \, \kappa^{d_z}(x, \sd z) \, \pi_0(\sd x) 
 < \infty,
\end{equation} something that holds trivially when $g_y^{d_z}(x,z)$ is bounded for all $ (x,z)\in \mX \times \mZ$.

Trough this section we assume that the observation $y\in\mY$ is arbitrary but fixed. More over we assume that the pdf $g_y^{d_z}(x,z)$ is only known up to a proportionality constant, and that it is bounded for $y\in \mY$, i.e. $$\|g_y^{d_z}\|_{\infty}=\sup_{(x,z)\in \mX \times \mZ} \abs{g_{y}^{d_z}(x,z)}< \infty.$$ 

\noindent The approximation of the posterior law $\pi(\sd x)$ for a given (fixed) observation $Y=y$ can be carried out using a simple nested IS scheme as shown in Section \ref{Algorithm}.

