\section{The Standard Case}\label{sec:standard_random}

In this section, we consider the standard Bayesian inference setting where we observe data \( Y \in \mathcal{Y} \) to infer a parameter \( X \in \mathcal{X} \), with a prior distribution \( \pi_0(\sd x) \) on \( \mathcal{X} \) and a likelihood function \( g(y|x) \). In our notation, the likelihood is denoted by \( g_y^{d_x}(x) \), where $d_x$ denotes the dimension of the parameter space \( \mathcal{X} \). This standard formulation can be viewed as a particular case of a more general model that might involve additional nuisance variables \( Z \in \mathcal{Z} \), where the full state is \((X, Z)\) and the likelihood depends on both, say \( g(y|x, z) \). In this simpler setting, the analysis developed in Section~\ref{sec:General_Random}  applies directly by setting the latent space \( \mathcal{Z} \) to be trivial or absent, and identifying the likelihood \( g_y^{d_x}(x) \) with the effective likelihood \( l_y^{d_x}(x) \).

\medskip
\noindent Under this standard setup, for a random observation \( Y \), the likelihood \( g_Y^{d_x}(x) \) is a real-valued random variable for each fixed \( x \in \mathcal{X} \). We assume \(g_y^{d_x} \in L^2(\pi_0)\) and satisfies the normalization condition
\[
\int_{\mathcal{Y}} g_y^{d_x}(x) \, \lambda (\sd y) = 1, \quad \forall x \in \mathbb{R}^{d_x},
\]
for the Lebesgue measure \( \lambda \) on \( \mathcal{Y} \).

For a test function \( \varphi \in L^2(\pi_0) \), the posterior random expectation is
\[
\pi_Y(\varphi) = \int_\mathcal{X} \varphi(x) \, \pi_Y(\sd x) = \frac{\int_\mathcal{X} \varphi(x)\, g_Y^{d_x}(x) \, \pi_0(\sd x)}{\int_\mathcal{X} g_Y^{d_x}(x') \, \pi_0(\sd x')}.
\]

\medskip
\noindent We define the probability space \( (\mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{Y}), \eta) \) governing the random observation \( Y \), where the measure \( \eta \) is
\[
\eta(\sd y) := \pi_0(g_y) \lambda(\sd y)
\]

\medskip\noindent The \emph{link function} maps observations to normalized likelihoods in \( L^2(\pi_0) \):
\[
\ell : \mathcal{Y} \to L^2(\pi_0), \quad y \mapsto \ell_y := \frac{g_y^{d_x}(\cdot)}{\int_\mathcal{X} g_y^{d_x}(x') \, \pi_0(\sd x')}.
\]
Hence, the  posterior expectation can then be expressed using the \( L^2(\pi_0) \) inner product
$$ \pi_Y(\varphi) = \left\langle \varphi, \ell_Y \right\rangle_{L^2(\pi_0)}.$$

\medskip
\noindent The boundedness of the functional \( \varphi \mapsto \mathbb{E}[\pi_Y^p(\varphi)] \) for \( \varphi \) in the Bochner space \( L^\infty(\mathcal{Y}; L^2(\pi_0)) \) is directly related to the Bochner integrability of the link function \( \ell_y \) with respect to the measure \( \eta \) and the space \( L^2(\pi_0) \). This requires \( \ell \) to be strongly \( \eta \)-measurable (which holds under mild regularity) and the finiteness of the norm integral
\[
\int_{\mathcal{Y}} \| \ell_y \|_{L^2(\pi_0)}^p \, \eta(\sd y) < \infty.
\]

\noindent Based on this, we can state the convergence result for the approximation of the posterior expectation in the standard case:

\begin{theorem}
Let \( g_y^{d_x}(x) \) be a strictly positive conditional pdf such that \( g_y^{d_x} \in L^2(\pi_0) \). Then, for any  \( f\in B(\mathcal{X})\) the following are equivalent
\begin{itemize}
    \item There exists a constant \( C_2^{d_x} < \infty \), independent of \( N \) and \( f \), such that
    \begin{equation}
        \left\| \pi_Y(f) - \pi_Y^{N}(f) \right\|_2 \leq \frac{C_2^{d_x} \| f \|_\infty}{\sqrt{N}}.
    \end{equation}
    \item The associated link function \( \ell \in L^2\big( \mathcal{Y}, L^2(\pi_0) \big) \).
\end{itemize}
Moreover, the constant $C_2^{d_x}$ satisfies 
\[
C_2^{d_x} \leq B_2 K_2^{d_x},
\]
where \( B_2 \) arise from the Marcinkiewicz–Zygmund inequality.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}\label{thIS2R_std}
Let \( g_y^{d_z}(x,z) \) satisfy the same conditions as in the previous theorem, and suppose the associated link function \( \ell \) belongs to the Bochner space \( L^p\big( \mathcal{Y}, L^2(\pi_0) \big) \) for some \( p \in \{1, 2\} \). Assume further that there exists a polynomial \( \tilde P_{n,p}(d_x) \) of degree \(n\), depending only on \(p\), such that
\begin{equation*}
K_p^{d_x} \leq \tilde P_{n,p}(d_x), \quad \forall d_x \in \mbN.
\end{equation*}
 Then, for any \( f \in B(\mX) \), there exists a polynomial \(P_{n,p}(d_x)\) of degree at most \(n\), depending only on \(p\), such that
\[
\left\| \pi_Y(f) - \pi_Y^{N}(f) \right\|_p 
\leq \frac{P_{n,p}(d_x)\, \|f\|_\infty}{\sqrt{N}}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
Under the assumptions of Theorem~\ref{thIS2R_std}, the number of samples needed to accurately approximate the posterior expectation \(\pi_Y(f)\) increases at most polynomially with the dimension \(d_x\) of the nuisance variables, rather than exponentially. This illustrates how structural properties of the model can effectively alleviate the curse of dimensionality in importance sampling.
\end{remark}


\begin{corollary}
Under the assumptions of Theorem~\ref{thIS2R_std}, suppose \( n = 0 \); that is, there exists a constant \( K_p > 0 \) such that
\begin{equation*}\label{eq:U_Kdz_std}
\sup_{d_x \in \mathbb{N}} K_p^{d_x} \leq K_p.
\end{equation*}
Then, for any \( f \in B(\mX) \), there exists a constant \( \mathcal{C}_p < \infty \), independent of \( N \), \( M \), \( f \), and the dimension \( d_x \), such that
\[
\left\| \pi_Y(f) - \pi_Y^{N}(f) \right\|_p 
\leq \frac{\mathcal{C}_p \, \|f\|_\infty}{\sqrt{N}}.
\]
In particular, $\lim_{N \to \infty} \| \pi_Y(f) - \pi_Y^{N,M}(f) \|_p = 0,$
uniformly in \(d_x\).
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The proof is nearly identical to its counterpart with nuisance variables; in fact, it is even more straightforward.
See Appendix~\ref{appThIS3}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Some particular cases in the Standard Setting}\label{sec:particular_cases_std}

In this section, we demonstrate how the assumptions of Theorem~\ref{thIS2R_std}, specifically the condition that the constant \( K_p^{d_x} = \int_{\mathcal{Y}} \left\| \ell_y \right\|_{L^2(\pi_0)}^p \, \eta(\sd y) \) is finite and uniformly bounded independently of the dimension \( d_x \), are satisfied in several common scenarios. These are presented as particular cases of the standard Bayesian model discussed in Section~\ref{sec:standard_random}.

\subsubsection{Linear Gaussian Case}

Consider the linear Gaussian model where the likelihood is given by \( g_y^{d_x}(x) = \mathcal{N}(Ax, R) \), where $A$ is a $d_y \times d_x$ matrix and $R$ is a $d_y \times d_y$ covariance matrix. The prior is \( \pi_0 \).

The analysis for the second moment of the \(L^2(\pi_0)\) norm of the link function, \( \mathbb{E}\left[\| \ell_Y \|_{L^2(\pi_0)}^2\right] \), follows the same steps as the linear Gaussian case in the context with nuisance variables (as presented in Section~\ref{sec:General_Random}), but with \( g_y^{d_x}(x) \) replacing \( l_y^{d_z}(x) \) and integration with respect to \( \pi_0 \) instead of \( \mix^{d_z} \).

The expectation \( \mathbb{E}\left[\| \ell_Y \|_{L^2(\pi_0)}^2\right] \) in this standard setting can be calculated explicitly and is finite, provided the resulting covariance matrices are positive definite. Ensuring this quantity, \( K_2^{d_x} \), remains uniformly bounded as \( d_x \) varies depends on how the matrices \( A \) and \( R \), and the prior \( \pi_0 \), scale with dimension.

\subsubsection{Bounded function observation}

Consider the observation model \( y = f(x) + \epsilon \), where \( \epsilon \sim \mathcal{N}(0, R) \) and \( f : \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}^{d_y} \) is a deterministic function uniformly bounded in Euclidean norm, i.e., \( \sup_{x\in\mathcal{X}}\norm{f(x)}_2 \leq M \), for some constant \( M > 0 \) independent of \( d_x \). The likelihood is \( g_y^{d_x}(x) = \mathcal{N}(y - f(x), R) \).

The analysis bounding the term \( \frac{g_y^{d_x}(x)^2}{\int_\mathcal{X} g_y^{d_x}(x') \, \pi_0(\sd x')} \) and showing the integrability of the dominant function with respect to \( \lambda(\sd y) \) mirrors the steps shown for the bounded function observation case in Section~\ref{sec:General_Random}. By replacing the joint space \( \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{Z} \) with \( \mathcal{X} \) and the function dependence on \((x,z)\) with dependence on \(x\), we find that the second moment \( \mathbb{E}\left[\| \ell_Y \|_{L^2(\pi_0)}^2\right] \) is bounded by a constant that depends on \( d_y \), \( R \), and \( M \), but is independent of \( d_x \).

This finiteness and independence from \( d_x \) implies that the constant \( K_2^{d_x} \) is uniformly bounded across dimensions, satisfying the assumptions of Theorem~\ref{thIS2R_std}.

\subsubsection{Bounded integrable likelihood}

Assume the likelihood function \( g_y^{d_x}(x) \) satisfies the pointwise bound
\[
g_y^{d_x}(x) \leq h(x)\, k(y),
\]
where \( h : \mathcal{X} \to [0, \infty) \) and \( k : \mathcal{Y} \to [0, \infty) \) are measurable functions such that \( \|h\|_{\infty} \leq M < \infty \) and \( \|k\|_{L^1(\lambda)} \leq K < \infty \), with constants \( M \) and \( K \) independent of the dimension \( d_x \).

Following the argument presented for the bounded integrable noise case in Section~\ref{sec:General_Random}, we can bound the second moment of the link function's norm:
\[
\mathbb{E}\left[\| \ell_Y \|_{L^2(\pi_0)}^2\right] \leq MK.
\]
Since \( M \) and \( K \) are independent of \( d_x \), the second moment \( K_2^{d_x} \) is finite and uniformly bounded across dimensions. This satisfies the condition for Theorem~\ref{thIS2R_std}.

