\begin{comment}
The results presented in this work can be naturally related to the analysis in \cite{agapiou2017importance}, where the authors investigate the impact of the state-space dimension \( d_x \), the data-space dimension \( d_y \), the nominal dimension \( \hat{d} = d_x \vee d_y \), and the intrinsic dimension (defined in the linear-Gaussian setting via the eigenstructure of the model matrices). In particular, they show that the chi-squared divergence \( \chi^2(\pi, \mu) \) between the proposal and the target distributions (which corresponds to the second moment of the Radon--Nikodym derivative of the posterior with respect to the prior; see Remark~\ref{rem:link_norm}), and thus the associated variance factor \( \rho = 1 + \chi^2(\pi, \mu) \), can grow exponentially with the nominal dimension \( \hat{d} \), even in the linear-Gaussian filtering case (see Table~I and Section~4.4 in \cite{agapiou2017importance}).
In contrast, our analysis adopts a different perspective by explicitly distinguishing between parameters of interest and nuisance variables. We consider a setting in which a fixed-dimensional subset of the state variables is of inferential relevance, and the data-space dimension is also held fixed, while the dimension \( d_z \) of the nuisance variables is allowed to grow. Under this formulation, we establish approximation error bounds for nested importance sampling estimators that grow at most polynomially with \( d_z \), both in the classical setting with fixed observations and, under Bochner integrability conditions, in the setting with random observations.
In the linear-Gaussian case, uniform error bounds can be obtained by controlling the eigenvalues and singular values of the model matrices, thereby indirectly constraining the intrinsic dimension as defined in \cite{agapiou2017importance}. These results suggest that, by carefully structuring the inference problem and exploiting the regularity of the model, one can mitigate the exponential performance degradation typically associated with the curse of dimensionality. In particular, this mitigation is achieved via spectral control in the fixed-observation setting and via integrability conditions on the link function in the random-observation setting.

Future research directions include extensions to dynamic models and generalizations to arbitrary values of \( p \) in the random observation setting. Further extensions may also explore broader classes of observation noise.


