\subsection{Stochastic Interpolants}

Stochastic Interpolants are processes that turn noise sampled from a simple Gaussian prior $x_1\sim \mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I})$ to data $x_0 \sim p_*(x) $. The time dependent process is as follows:

\begin{equation}
    \label{interpolant}
    x_t = \alpha_t x_0 + \sigma_tx_1
\end{equation}

Here, $\alpha_t$ is a decreasing function of $t$ and $\sigma_t$ is a increasing function of $t$. The process is restricted on $t \in [0,1]$ such that $x_t$ is exactly $x_1$ at $t=1$ ($\alpha_1 = 0, \sigma_1 = 1)$ and $x_0$ at $t=0$ ($\alpha_0 = 1, \sigma_0 = 0)$.

The sample $x_t$ under the stochastic interpolant evolves according to a vector field:

\begin{equation}
    \label{SI ODE}
    \frac{d x_t}{dt} = v(t,x_t),
\end{equation}


%the vector field and score function stuff can also go in the supplementary to make space in case we need it
where the vector field is given by the following conditional expectation:

\begin{equation}
    \label{SI vector field}
    v(t,x) = \dot{\alpha_t}\mathbb{E}\left[x_0|x_t=x\right] + \dot{\sigma_t}\mathbb{E}\left[x_1|x_t=x\right]
\end{equation}

The score function, $s(t,x) = \nabla \log p_t(x)$, of the probability flow ODE associated with the interpolant is given by the following conditional expectation:

\begin{equation}
    \label{SI score function}
    s(t,x) = \sigma_t^{-1}\mathbb{E} \left[ x_1 |x_t=x \right]
\end{equation}

Given a coupling of the prior and data distribution $C(x_0,x_1)$, we can learn the vector field and score function through the following objective functions:

\begin{equation}
    \label{vector field objective SI}
    \mathcal{L}_v = \mathbb{E}_{t \sim \mathcal{U}(0,1),\, (x_0,x_1) \sim C(x_0,x_1)}\left[ \|v_\theta(t,x_t) - \dot{\alpha_t} x_0 - \dot{\sigma_t} x_1 \|^2\right]
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
    \label{score function objective SI}
    \mathcal{L}_s = \mathbb{E}_{t \sim \mathcal{U}(0,1),\, (x_0,x_1) \sim C(x_0,x_1)}\left[ \|\sigma_ts_\theta(t,x_t) +  x_1 \|^2\right]
\end{equation}

The vector field objective (Eq.~\ref{vector field objective SI}) can be further modified so that the model is trained to predict the final endpoint $x_0$ instead of the vector field. Through simple algebraic rearrangement, it can be shown that the objective function~\ref{vector field objective SI} is equivalent to the following endpoint objective:

\begin{equation}
    \label{endpoint objective SI}
    \mathcal{L}_{EP} = \mathbb{E}_{t \sim \mathcal{U}(0,1),\, (x_0,x_1) \sim C(x_0,x_1)} \left[\| \frac{\dot{\alpha_t}\sigma_t - \alpha_t}{\sigma_t}  (\hat{x}_0(t,x_t) - x_0)\|^2 \right]
\end{equation}

Where $\hat{x}_0(t,x_t)$ is the predicted endpoint by the neural network model. For more detail, refer section~\ref{section: endpoint objective}. In this work we use both the vector field (Eq.~\ref{vector field objective SI}) and endpoint (Eq.~\ref{endpoint objective SI}) objectives to train our CNF models.

