\section{Energy Based Model training with InfoNCE and Score Matching}

%can directly start with the time dependant parametrization to make space later if needed
Enery Based Models parametrize the density function as proportional to the exponential of a learned energy function $E_\theta(x)$ as follows:

\begin{equation}
\label{ebm}
    p_\theta = \frac{\exp(E_\theta(x))}{Z_\theta},\quad Z_\theta = \int \exp(E_\theta(x)) dx
\end{equation}

We can also make the density function time-dependant when the samples $x_t$ evolves according to a time process (e.g. with stochastic interpolants)

\begin{equation}
\label{ebm-time}
    p_\theta(t,x_t) = \frac{\exp(E_\theta(t,x_t))}{Z_\theta(t)}, \quad Z_\theta(t) = \int \exp(E_\theta(t,x)) dx
\end{equation}

Given access to samples from a simple prior distribution $x_1 \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I})$, samples from a data distribution $x_0 \sim p_*(x)$ and a coupling function $C(x_0,x_1)$, we can obtain sample $x_t$ at time point $t$ with stochastic interpolants, as given by Eq.\ref{interpolant}.  

To maximize the likelihood of pair $(t,x_t)$ occurring under the distribution $p_\theta$ modeled by the energy based model, we minimize the negative log likelihood given by:

\begin{equation}
    \label{NLL objective}
    \mathcal{L_{\text{NLL}}} = \sum_{i=1}^N -\log \frac{\exp(E_\theta(t^i,x_t^i))}{\int \exp(E_\theta(t,x_t^i))dt}
\end{equation}

The intractable integral in the denominator can be approximated by appropriately sampling a set of \emph{negative} time points $\{\tilde{t}^i\}$ yielding the InfoNCE loss given by:

\begin{equation}
    \label{InfoNCE}
    \mathcal{L}_{\text{InfoNCE}} = \sum_{i=1}^N -\log \frac{\exp(E_\theta(t^i,x_t^i))} {\sum_{t' \in \{\tilde{t}^i\} \cup t^i } {\exp(E_\theta(t',x_t^i))}}
\end{equation}

Note that this objective function is \emph{simulation-free} as it only requires sampling of negative time points. Furthermore, the gradient of the energy function is also the score of the model's density $\nabla \log p_\theta(t,x_t) = \nabla E_\theta(t,x_t)$, therefore we can use the well defined score matching objective (Eq.~\ref{score function objective SI}) associated with stochastic interpolants as an additional objective to train the EBM as follows:

\begin{equation}
    \label{EBM SM}
    \mathcal{L}_{\text{SM}} = \mathbb{E}_{t \sim \mathcal{U}(0,1),\, (x_0,x_1) \sim C(x_0,x_1)}\left[ \|\sigma_t\nabla E_\theta(t,x_t) +  x_1 \|^2\right]
\end{equation}

Where $C(x_0,x_1)$ is a coupling function, and $x_t$ is computed using Eq.~\ref{interpolant}. We show that both objectives are important for optimal model performance on toy 2D systems in Figure~\ref{2D systems} and therefore use both loss functions for subsequent experiments on molecular systems.

