\section{Jacobian matrix}\label{ap:jacobian}

The Jacobian matrix $J$ is defined from the partial derivatives of Eq. (\ref{eq:ikeda2d}) as

\[
J = \begin{bmatrix}
\frac{\partial x_{n+1}}{\partial x_n} & \frac{\partial x_{n+1}}{\partial y_n} \\
\frac{\partial y_{n+1}}{\partial x_n} & \frac{\partial y_{n+1}}{\partial y_n}
\end{bmatrix},
\]

where

\begin{align}
\frac{\partial x_{n+1}}{\partial x_n} &= B \left( \cos t_n - (x_n \sin t_n + y_n \cos t_n) \frac{\partial t_n}{\partial x_n} \right),  \\
\frac{\partial x_{n+1}}{\partial y_n} &= B \left( -\sin t_n - (x_n \sin t_n + y_n \cos t_n) \frac{\partial t_n}{\partial y_n} \right),  \\
\frac{\partial y_{n+1}}{\partial x_n} &= B \left( \sin t_n + (x_n \cos t_n - y_n \sin t_n) \frac{\partial t_n}{\partial x_n} \right),  \\
\frac{\partial y_{n+1}}{\partial y_n} &= B \left( \cos t_n + (x_n \cos t_n - y_n \sin t_n) \frac{\partial t_n}{\partial y_n} \right), 
\end{align}

\noindent and $\frac{\partial t_n}{\partial x_n}$ and $\frac{\partial t_n}{\partial y_n}$ are

\begin{align}
\frac{\partial t_n}{\partial x_n} = \frac{2\phi y_n}{(1 + x_n^2 + y_n^2)^2}, \\
\frac{\partial t_n}{\partial y_n} = \frac{2\phi x_n}{(1 + x_n^2 + y_n^2)^2}.
\end{align}

It is straightforward to check that

\begin{align}
    \det J = \frac{\partial x_{n+1}}{\partial x_n}\frac{\partial y_{n+1}}{\partial y_n}-\frac{\partial x_{n+1}}{\partial y_n}\frac{\partial y_{n+1}}{\partial x_n}=B^2.
\end{align}

